PathwayData.put("data/reading_writing/units/L07U1.json",{"strand":"reading_writing","level":7,"cefr":"B1+","unit":1,"theme":"Identity and Belonging","essential_question":"What shapes who we are?","schema_version":"1.0","core_words":["identity","belong","roots","values","personality","influence","upbringing","unique","generation","ancestor","proud","background","faith","custom"],"recycled_words":["family","tradition","culture","theme","character","change","simile","metaphor","narrative","sensory details","dialogue","feelings","memory"],"rubrics":[{"id":"RW-RUBRIC-5T","title":"Analytic writing rubric","traits":[{"name":"Ideas","name_ar":"الأفكار","bands":[{"score":4,"descriptor":"My ideas are clear, interesting and fully developed with strong details.","descriptor_ar":"أفكاري واضحة وممتعة ومدعومة بتفاصيل قوية."},{"score":3,"descriptor":"My ideas are clear and have enough details.","descriptor_ar":"أفكاري واضحة ومعها تفاصيل كافية."},{"score":2,"descriptor":"My main idea is there but needs more details.","descriptor_ar":"فكرتي الرئيسية موجودة لكنها تحتاج إلى تفاصيل أكثر."},{"score":1,"descriptor":"My ideas are unclear or very few.","descriptor_ar":"أفكاري غير واضحة أو قليلة جدًا."}]},{"name":"Organization","name_ar":"التنظيم","bands":[{"score":4,"descriptor":"Clear beginning, middle and end; ideas flow with linking words.","descriptor_ar":"بداية ووسط ونهاية واضحة، والأفكار مترابطة بكلمات الربط."},{"score":3,"descriptor":"Clear order with some linking words.","descriptor_ar":"ترتيب واضح مع بعض كلمات الربط."},{"score":2,"descriptor":"Some order, but parts are mixed up.","descriptor_ar":"يوجد بعض الترتيب لكن بعض الأجزاء غير مرتبة."},{"score":1,"descriptor":"Ideas are not in order.","descriptor_ar":"الأفكار غير مرتبة."}]},{"name":"Vocabulary","name_ar":"المفردات","bands":[{"score":4,"descriptor":"I use exact, varied words, including new unit words, correctly.","descriptor_ar":"أستخدم كلمات دقيقة ومتنوعة، منها كلمات الوحدة الجديدة، بشكل صحيح."},{"score":3,"descriptor":"I use some new words correctly.","descriptor_ar":"أستخدم بعض الكلمات الجديدة بشكل صحيح."},{"score":2,"descriptor":"I use simple words; some are repeated or wrong.","descriptor_ar":"أستخدم كلمات بسيطة، وبعضها مكرر أو غير صحيح."},{"score":1,"descriptor":"I use very few words; the meaning is hard to understand.","descriptor_ar":"أستخدم كلمات قليلة جدًا ويصعب فهم المعنى."}]},{"name":"Sentence fluency","name_ar":"سلاسة الجمل","bands":[{"score":4,"descriptor":"My sentences are complete, varied in length and easy to read aloud.","descriptor_ar":"جملي كاملة ومتنوعة الطول وسهلة القراءة بصوت عالٍ."},{"score":3,"descriptor":"Most sentences are complete, with some variety.","descriptor_ar":"معظم جملي كاملة مع بعض التنويع."},{"score":2,"descriptor":"Many sentences are short or start the same way.","descriptor_ar":"كثير من جملي قصيرة أو تبدأ بالطريقة نفسها."},{"score":1,"descriptor":"Sentences are incomplete or run together.","descriptor_ar":"جملي ناقصة أو متداخلة."}]},{"name":"Conventions","name_ar":"قواعد الكتابة","bands":[{"score":4,"descriptor":"Capitals, punctuation, spelling and grammar are almost always correct.","descriptor_ar":"الحروف الكبيرة وعلامات الترقيم والإملاء والقواعد صحيحة دائمًا تقريبًا."},{"score":3,"descriptor":"A few mistakes that do not affect meaning.","descriptor_ar":"أخطاء قليلة لا تؤثر في المعنى."},{"score":2,"descriptor":"Several mistakes; the meaning is sometimes unclear.","descriptor_ar":"عدة أخطاء، والمعنى أحيانًا غير واضح."},{"score":1,"descriptor":"Many mistakes make it hard to read.","descriptor_ar":"أخطاء كثيرة تجعل القراءة صعبة."}]}]}],"lessons":[{"id":"RW-L07-U1-LS1","title":"Word Power: Identity and Belonging","lesson_type":"vocabulary","objectives":["read, say and use 14 words about identity and belonging and 5 Tier 2 words (reflect, inherit, embrace, transform, perceive)","distinguish denotation (dictionary meaning) from connotation (the feelings a word carries)","choose words with positive, neutral or negative connotations to match a purpose"],"objectives_ar":["قراءة ونطق واستخدام 14 كلمة عن الهوية والانتماء و5 كلمات أكاديمية.","التمييز بين المعنى المعجمي (الدلالة الصريحة) والإيحاء (المشاعر التي تحملها الكلمة).","اختيار كلمات ذات إيحاءات إيجابية أو محايدة أو سلبية بما يناسب الغرض."],"success_criteria":["I can use the identity words correctly in sentences.","I can explain the denotation and the connotation of a word.","I can sort word sets from positive to negative and explain how a writer's word choice shapes the reader's feelings."],"success_criteria_ar":["أستطيع استخدام كلمات الهوية في جمل بشكل صحيح.","أستطيع شرح الدلالة الصريحة والإيحاء لكلمة ما.","أستطيع ترتيب مجموعات الكلمات من الإيجابي إلى السلبي وشرح أثر اختيار الكاتب في مشاعر القارئ."],"map_area":"Vocabulary: Acquisition and Use","vocabulary":[{"word":"identity","pos":"noun","ipa":"/aɪˈdɛntəti/","definition":"who a person is: the things that make someone special and different","arabic":"هوية","example":"My language is part of my identity.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L07-U1-LS1-V01","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L07-U1-LS1-V01","tier":"core"},{"word":"belong","pos":"verb","ipa":"/bɪˈlɔŋ/","definition":"to feel happy and accepted in a place or a group","arabic":"ينتمي","example":"I belong to a big, loving family.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L07-U1-LS1-V02","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L07-U1-LS1-V02","tier":"core"},{"word":"roots","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ruts/","definition":"the family, place and history that a person comes from","arabic":"جذور / أصول","example":"My roots are in a small village in Asir.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L07-U1-LS1-V03","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L07-U1-LS1-V03","tier":"core"},{"word":"values","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈvæljuz/","definition":"the beliefs about what is important and right","arabic":"قيم","example":"Honesty and generosity are family values.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L07-U1-LS1-V04","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L07-U1-LS1-V04","tier":"core"},{"word":"personality","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˌpɝsəˈnæləti/","definition":"the type of person you are: how you think, feel and behave","arabic":"شخصية","example":"She has a cheerful personality.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L07-U1-LS1-V05","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L07-U1-LS1-V05","tier":"core"},{"word":"influence","pos":"noun / verb","ipa":"/ˈɪnfluəns/","definition":"the power to change how someone thinks or acts; to have this power","arabic":"تأثير / يؤثر","example":"My grandfather had a strong influence on me.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L07-U1-LS1-V06","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L07-U1-LS1-V06","tier":"core"},{"word":"upbringing","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈʌpˌbrɪŋɪŋ/","definition":"the way parents teach and care for a child as he or she grows up","arabic":"تنشئة / تربية","example":"Her upbringing taught her to respect elders.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L07-U1-LS1-V07","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L07-U1-LS1-V07","tier":"core"},{"word":"unique","pos":"adjective","ipa":"/juˈnik/","definition":"the only one of its kind; very special","arabic":"فريد","example":"Every fingerprint is unique.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L07-U1-LS1-V08","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L07-U1-LS1-V08","tier":"core"},{"word":"generation","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˌdʒɛnəˈreɪʃən/","definition":"all the people born at about the same time; one step in a family line","arabic":"جيل","example":"Three generations live in our house.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L07-U1-LS1-V09","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L07-U1-LS1-V09","tier":"core"},{"word":"ancestor","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈænˌsɛstər/","definition":"a family member who lived long before your grandparents","arabic":"سَلَف / جدّ","example":"My ancestors were traders on the incense route.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L07-U1-LS1-V10","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L07-U1-LS1-V10","tier":"core"},{"word":"proud","pos":"adjective","ipa":"/praʊd/","definition":"feeling pleased about something you or people close to you have done or are","arabic":"فخور","example":"I am proud of my country.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L07-U1-LS1-V11","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L07-U1-LS1-V11","tier":"core"},{"word":"background","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈbækˌɡraʊnd/","definition":"a person's family, culture, education and experience","arabic":"خلفية","example":"Students in our class come from different backgrounds.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L07-U1-LS1-V12","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L07-U1-LS1-V12","tier":"core"},{"word":"faith","pos":"noun","ipa":"/feɪθ/","definition":"strong belief, especially religious belief","arabic":"إيمان","example":"Faith gives many people strength.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L07-U1-LS1-V13","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L07-U1-LS1-V13","tier":"core"},{"word":"custom","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈkʌstəm/","definition":"a traditional way of doing something in a culture","arabic":"عادة / عُرف","example":"Serving coffee to guests is a Saudi custom.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L07-U1-LS1-V14","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L07-U1-LS1-V14","tier":"core"},{"word":"reflect","pos":"verb","ipa":"/rɪˈflɛkt/","definition":"to think carefully about something; to show something","arabic":"يتأمل / يعكس","example":"I reflected on what my grandmother said.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L07-U1-LS1-V15","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L07-U1-LS1-V15","tier":"tier2"},{"word":"inherit","pos":"verb","ipa":"/ɪnˈhɛrɪt/","definition":"to receive something from a family member who lived before you","arabic":"يرث","example":"She inherited her grandmother's dallah.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L07-U1-LS1-V16","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L07-U1-LS1-V16","tier":"tier2"},{"word":"embrace","pos":"verb","ipa":"/ɪmˈbreɪs/","definition":"to accept something happily; to hug","arabic":"يتقبّل / يحتضن","example":"He learned to embrace his family's traditions.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L07-U1-LS1-V17","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L07-U1-LS1-V17","tier":"tier2"},{"word":"transform","pos":"verb","ipa":"/trænsˈfɔrm/","definition":"to change something completely","arabic":"يحوّل","example":"The trip transformed the way I see my roots.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L07-U1-LS1-V18","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L07-U1-LS1-V18","tier":"tier2"},{"word":"perceive","pos":"verb","ipa":"/pərˈsiv/","definition":"to understand or think of something in a particular way","arabic":"يدرك / ينظر إلى","example":"I used to perceive old things as boring.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L07-U1-LS1-V19","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L07-U1-LS1-V19","tier":"tier2"}],"explanation":{"text":"Every word has a **denotation** – its **dictionary meaning**. Many words also have a **connotation** – the **feelings or ideas** that come with the word. A connotation can be **positive** 😊, **neutral** 😐 or **negative** 🙁.\n\nWords with the **same denotation** can have **different connotations**: *old* (neutral) / *antique* (positive – valuable) / *worn-out* (negative). *Determined* (positive) / *stubborn* (negative). *Curious* (positive) / *nosy* (negative). *Home* (warm feelings) / *house* (just a building). *Traditional* (positive) / *old-fashioned* (often negative).\n\nWriters choose words carefully to make readers **feel** something. Ask: (1) What does the word **mean**? (2) What **feeling** does it carry? (3) **Why** did the writer choose it instead of a synonym? When you write, choose the connotation that fits your **purpose**.","examples":["*old* 😐 → *antique* 😊 → *worn-out* 🙁 – the same age, different feelings.","*determined* 😊 vs. *stubborn* 🙁 – both mean 'not changing your mind'.","*curious* 😊 vs. *nosy* 🙁 – both mean 'wanting to know'.","*confident* 😊 vs. *arrogant* 🙁 – both mean 'sure of yourself'.","*home* (love, family, safety) vs. *house* (a building).","*traditional* 😊 vs. *old-fashioned* 🙁: *My grandmother's **traditional** dallah* vs. *that **old-fashioned** pot*.","*proud* can be 😊 (*proud of my roots*) or 🙁 (*too proud to ask for help*) – the context decides.","*unique* 😊 vs. *strange* 🙁 vs. *different* 😐.","Writer's choice: *Her eyes **sparkled** as she told the story* (warm) vs. *Her eyes **glinted*** (can feel cold or sharp)."],"common_errors":["✗ Thinking synonyms always mean exactly the same. → ✓ Check the connotation: *thrifty* ≠ *stingy*. (Same meaning, different feeling.)","✗ My grandmother is very cheap. → ✓ My grandmother is very careful with money (thrifty). (Avoid negative words for people you respect.)","✗ Calling a heritage object 'junk'. → ✓ Call it *antique*, *precious* or *treasured*. (Match the feeling to your purpose.)","✗ He is proud on his roots. → ✓ He is proud of his roots. (proud **of**.)","✗ I belong in this family to. → ✓ I belong to this family. (belong **to** a group.)","✗ Ignoring context: proud is always good. → ✓ Check the sentence: too proud = negative. (Context decides.)","✗ Using a dictionary for meaning only. → ✓ Ask also: what feeling does it carry? (Denotation + connotation.)"],"arabic":"لكل كلمة دلالة صريحة هي معناها في المعجم، ولكثير من الكلمات إيحاء هو المشاعر أو الأفكار المصاحبة لها، وقد يكون إيجابيًا أو محايدًا أو سلبيًا.\n\nقد تتفق كلمات في المعنى المعجمي وتختلف في الإيحاء: old محايدة، وantique إيجابية، وworn-out سلبية. وdetermined إيجابية، وstubborn سلبية. وcurious إيجابية، وnosy سلبية.\n\nيختار الكتّاب كلماتهم ليجعلوا القارئ يشعر بشيء معين؛ فاسأل: ما معنى الكلمة؟ وما الشعور الذي تحمله؟ ولماذا اختارها الكاتب بدل مرادفها؟\n\nانتبه: proud of وbelong to، والسياق يحدد إن كانت proud إيجابية أو سلبية."},"video":{"script":[{"time":"00:00–00:30","narration":"Who are you? Your name, your family, your faith, your language… What shapes who we are?","on_screen":"Identity and Belonging 🌴","visual":"A mosaic of faces, family photos, a dallah and a palm tree."},{"time":"00:30–01:30","narration":"Identity, belong, roots, values, personality, influence, upbringing, unique, generation, ancestor, proud, background, faith, custom!","on_screen":"identity · belong · roots · values · personality · influence · upbringing · unique · generation · ancestor · proud · background · faith · custom","visual":"Each word appears on a branch of a family tree."},{"time":"01:30–02:10","narration":"We inherit, we reflect, we embrace – and sometimes the way we perceive our roots is transformed!","on_screen":"reflect · inherit · embrace · transform · perceive","visual":"An old object passes from one generation to the next."},{"time":"02:10–03:30","narration":"Old, antique, worn-out – the same meaning but different feelings! That's denotation and connotation.","on_screen":"Denotation = meaning · Connotation = feeling","visual":"A brass pot labeled three ways with smiling, neutral and frowning faces."},{"time":"03:30–04:00","narration":"Choose your words carefully – they carry feelings to your reader!","on_screen":"Choose the connotation that fits","visual":"A writer picks 'antique' and the pot glows."}],"captions_ar":["من أنت؟ اسمك وعائلتك وإيمانك ولغتك… ما الذي يشكّل هويتنا؟","هوية، ينتمي، جذور، قيم، شخصية، تأثير، تنشئة، فريد، جيل، سلف، فخور، خلفية، إيمان، عادة!","نرث ونتأمل ونتقبّل، وأحيانًا تتحول نظرتنا إلى جذورنا!","قديم، عتيق، بالٍ: المعنى نفسه بمشاعر مختلفة. هذه هي الدلالة والإيحاء.","اختر كلماتك بعناية، فهي تحمل المشاعر إلى قارئك!"],"voice":"Narrator: American English, warm, thoughtful female voice, speed 1.0."},"texts":[],"activities":[{"id":"RW-L07-U1-LS1-A1","type":"mcq","stage":"controlled","instructions":"Controlled practice: Choose the correct identity word.","instructions_ar":"اختر كلمة الهوية الصحيحة.","items":[{"q":"My grandfather, my father and I are three different ___ of the same family.","answer":"generations","explanation":"Generation.","explanation_ar":"أجيال.","options":["customs","personalities","generations","backgrounds"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"Serving dates with Arabic coffee to guests is an old Saudi ___.","answer":"custom","explanation":"Custom.","explanation_ar":"عادة.","options":["ancestor","custom","identity","upbringing"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"Our family's ___ are honesty, generosity and respect for elders.","answer":"values","explanation":"Values.","explanation_ar":"قيم.","options":["roots","ancestors","generations","values"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"Many centuries ago, my ___ traded spices along the coast.","answer":"ancestors","explanation":"Ancestor.","explanation_ar":"أسلافي.","options":["ancestors","personalities","customs","values"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"She is calm, funny and kind. She has a lovely ___.","answer":"personality","explanation":"Personality.","explanation_ar":"شخصية.","options":["personality","background","generation","custom"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"The new student didn't know anyone at first, but now she feels that she truly ___ in our class.","answer":"belongs","explanation":"Belong.","explanation_ar":"تنتمي.","options":["inherits","transforms","belongs","perceives"],"dok":2,"skill":"vocabulary","stage":"controlled"}]},{"id":"RW-L07-U1-LS1-A2","type":"mcq","stage":"guided","instructions":"Guided practice: Denotation and connotation. Choose.","instructions_ar":"الدلالة والإيحاء: اختر.","items":[{"q":"Which word has the MOST positive connotation for an old object?","answer":"antique","explanation":"Connotation.","explanation_ar":"antique.","options":["old","worn-out","used","antique"],"dok":1,"skill":"connotation","stage":"guided"},{"q":"Which word has a NEGATIVE connotation for someone who asks many questions?","answer":"nosy","explanation":"Connotation.","explanation_ar":"nosy.","options":["nosy","curious","interested","eager"],"dok":2,"skill":"connotation","stage":"guided"},{"q":"*Determined* and *stubborn* have a similar denotation. How are they different?","answer":"Determined sounds positive; stubborn sounds negative.","explanation":"Denotation vs. connotation.","explanation_ar":"مختلفتان في الإيحاء.","options":["They are exactly the same.","Determined sounds positive; stubborn sounds negative.","Stubborn sounds positive; determined sounds negative.","They have opposite meanings."],"dok":2,"skill":"connotation","stage":"guided"},{"q":"Which sentence uses *proud* with a NEGATIVE connotation?","answer":"He was too proud to say sorry.","explanation":"Context.","explanation_ar":"إيحاء سلبي.","options":["She is proud of her roots.","We are proud of our team.","I felt proud when I finished.","He was too proud to say sorry."],"dok":2,"skill":"connotation","stage":"guided"},{"q":"Which sentence about my grandmother is correct?","answer":"I am proud of my grandmother's kindness.","explanation":"proud of.","explanation_ar":"صحيحة.","options":["I am proud on my grandmother's kindness.","I am proud for my grandmother's kindness.","I am proud of my grandmother's kindness.","I am proud at my grandmother's kindness."],"dok":2,"skill":"grammar","stage":"guided"},{"q":"A writer calls a family house *a warm home full of stories* instead of *a building*. Why?","answer":"to make readers feel love and belonging","explanation":"Writer's purpose.","explanation_ar":"ليشعر القارئ بالدفء والانتماء.","options":["to give its exact size","to make readers feel love and belonging","to describe its price","to show it is new"],"dok":3,"skill":"author's craft","stage":"guided"}]},{"id":"RW-L07-U1-LS1-A3","type":"gap_fill","stage":"freer","instructions":"Freer practice: Use the word bank. Each word fits one sentence.","instructions_ar":"استخدم بنك الكلمات.","items":[{"q":"After the trip to the village, she began to ________ on what home really means.","answer":"reflect","explanation":"Think carefully.","explanation_ar":"تتأمل.","stage":"freer"},{"q":"He ________ his grandfather's love of poetry.","answer":"inherited","explanation":"Received from family.","explanation_ar":"ورث.","stage":"freer"},{"q":"It took time, but Faris learned to ________ his two cultures.","answer":"embrace","explanation":"Accept happily.","explanation_ar":"يتقبّل.","stage":"freer"},{"q":"Living abroad for a year ________ the way she saw her own country.","answer":"transformed","explanation":"Changed completely.","explanation_ar":"حوّل.","stage":"freer"},{"q":"As a child, I used to ________ my grandmother's stories as boring.","answer":"perceive","explanation":"Think of.","explanation_ar":"أرى / أعدّ.","stage":"freer"},{"q":"My Saudi ________ and my school abroad both shaped who I am.","answer":"upbringing","explanation":"How I was raised.","explanation_ar":"تنشئتي.","stage":"freer"}]},{"id":"RW-L07-U1-LS1-A4","type":"open","stage":"production","instructions":"Writing: Write with the new words.","instructions_ar":"اكتب باستخدام الكلمات الجديدة.","items":[{"q":"Describe an object from your home twice: once with positive connotations, once with negative ones.","answer":"Positive: Our antique wooden chest is full of treasured memories. Negative: Our old, scratched box is full of dusty junk.","explanation":"Connotation.","explanation_ar":"الإيحاء.","dok":2,"skill":"connotation","stage":"production"},{"q":"Write two sentences that show the difference between *curious* and *nosy*.","answer":"My curious brother asks questions to learn about science. My nosy neighbor asks questions about everyone's private business.","explanation":"Connotation.","explanation_ar":"الإيحاء.","dok":2,"skill":"connotation","stage":"production"},{"q":"Which three things are most important to your identity? Explain one.","answer":"My faith, my family and my language. My language is important because it connects me to my grandparents and their stories.","explanation":"Reflect.","explanation_ar":"تأمّل.","dok":2,"skill":"connect","stage":"production"},{"q":"Who has had the strongest influence on your values? Give an example.","answer":"My father has had the strongest influence on me; he taught me to keep my promises, even small ones.","explanation":"Reflect.","explanation_ar":"تأمّل.","dok":3,"skill":"connect","stage":"production"}]}],"game":{"name":"Connotation Thermometer","mechanic":"timed_quiz","rules":["On this screen: answer the lesson questions as fast as you can.","In class: Thermometer: the child drags words onto a thermometer from cold to warm, then chooses the best word for a writer's purpose (for example, to praise a grandmother's dallah). Correct choices raise the temperature.","Teams draw a word set.","They place the words on the thermometer and explain the order.","The teacher gives a purpose card (praise, criticize, describe neutrally).","Teams choose the best word for the purpose and use it in a sentence.","Correct order = 2 points; correct word for the purpose = 1 point."],"data":[]},"exit_quiz":[{"q":"Which word means *who a person is: the things that make someone special*?","answer":"identity","explanation":"Identity.","explanation_ar":"هوية.","options":["custom","generation","background","identity"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary"},{"q":"What is the *denotation* of a word?","answer":"its dictionary meaning","explanation":"Definition.","explanation_ar":"المعنى المعجمي.","options":["the feeling it carries","its dictionary meaning","its spelling","its pronunciation"],"dok":1,"skill":"denotation"},{"q":"*She **inherited** her grandmother's love of cooking.* *Inherited* means ___.","answer":"received from a family member before her","explanation":"Context.","explanation_ar":"ورثت.","options":["received from a family member before her","lost","bought","forgot"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary in context"},{"q":"What is the *connotation* of a word?","answer":"the feeling or idea that comes with it","explanation":"Definition.","explanation_ar":"الإيحاء.","options":["its dictionary meaning only","its number of letters","the feeling or idea that comes with it","its opposite"],"dok":1,"skill":"connotation"},{"q":"Which sentence about belonging is correct?","answer":"I belong to a big family in Taif.","explanation":"belong to.","explanation_ar":"صحيحة.","options":["I belong at a big family in Taif.","I belong to a big family in Taif.","I belongs to a big family in Taif.","I am belong to a big family in Taif."],"dok":2,"skill":"grammar"},{"q":"Which word gives the MOST negative picture of someone who is careful with money?","answer":"stingy","explanation":"Connotation.","explanation_ar":"stingy.","options":["thrifty","stingy","economical","careful"],"dok":2,"skill":"connotation"},{"q":"*Her **old-fashioned** dress* and *her **traditional** dress* – how is the feeling different?","answer":"Traditional sounds respectful; old-fashioned can sound negative.","explanation":"Connotation.","explanation_ar":"traditional تحمل احترامًا.","options":["They feel exactly the same.","Old-fashioned sounds more positive.","Traditional sounds negative.","Traditional sounds respectful; old-fashioned can sound negative."],"dok":2,"skill":"connotation"},{"q":"Which word has a NEUTRAL connotation?","answer":"different","explanation":"Neutral.","explanation_ar":"different.","options":["different","unique","weird","special"],"dok":2,"skill":"connotation"},{"q":"A writer wants readers to admire a grandmother's coffee pot. Which description is BEST?","answer":"a gleaming brass dallah that has welcomed guests for sixty years","explanation":"Purpose.","explanation_ar":"الأفضل.","options":["a dented old metal pot","a pot","a gleaming brass dallah that has welcomed guests for sixty years","a heavy, ugly thing on the shelf"],"dok":3,"skill":"author's craft"},{"q":"Why is it important to notice connotations when you read?","answer":"They show how the writer wants you to feel and can reveal the writer's attitude.","explanation":"Purpose.","explanation_ar":"تكشف موقف الكاتب.","options":["They make words shorter.","They are only used in dictionaries.","They always mean the opposite.","They show how the writer wants you to feel and can reveal the writer's attitude."],"dok":3,"skill":"connotation"}],"images":[{"id":"IMG-RW-L07-U1-LS1-V01","prompt":"Picture for the word 'identity': a girl looking into a mirror with family photos around it"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L07-U1-LS1-V02","prompt":"Picture for the word 'belong': a child smiling in a circle of friends"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L07-U1-LS1-V03","prompt":"Picture for the word 'roots': a tree with deep roots and a house"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L07-U1-LS1-V04","prompt":"Picture for the word 'values': a heart, a handshake and scales"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L07-U1-LS1-V05","prompt":"Picture for the word 'personality': a smiling face with a speech bubble full of stars"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L07-U1-LS1-V06","prompt":"Picture for the word 'influence': an older man guiding a child's hand"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L07-U1-LS1-V07","prompt":"Picture for the word 'upbringing': parents helping a child with homework"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L07-U1-LS1-V08","prompt":"Picture for the word 'unique': one colorful fish in a group of gray fish"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L07-U1-LS1-V09","prompt":"Picture for the word 'generation': a grandmother, a mother and a daughter"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L07-U1-LS1-V10","prompt":"Picture for the word 'ancestor': old portraits on a family tree"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L07-U1-LS1-V11","prompt":"Picture for the word 'proud': a boy holding a certificate with a big smile"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L07-U1-LS1-V12","prompt":"Picture for the word 'background': children from different countries together"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L07-U1-LS1-V13","prompt":"Picture for the word 'faith': a quiet mosque at sunrise"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L07-U1-LS1-V14","prompt":"Picture for the word 'custom': a host pouring Arabic coffee"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L07-U1-LS1-V15","prompt":"Picture for the word 'reflect': a girl thinking by a window"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L07-U1-LS1-V16","prompt":"Picture for the word 'inherit': an old object passed from one hand to another"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L07-U1-LS1-V17","prompt":"Picture for the word 'embrace': a child hugging a grandparent"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L07-U1-LS1-V18","prompt":"Picture for the word 'transform': a caterpillar becoming a butterfly"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L07-U1-LS1-V19","prompt":"Picture for the word 'perceive': a pair of glasses showing two different views"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L07-U1-LS1-01","prompt":"Three generations sharing coffee and family stories."},{"id":"IMG-RW-L07-U1-LS1-02","prompt":"Icons for identity words."},{"id":"IMG-RW-L07-U1-LS1-03","prompt":"A thermometer showing word connotations."}],"audio":[{"id":"AUD-RW-L07-U1-LS1-V01","script":"identity. My language is part of my identity."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L07-U1-LS1-V02","script":"belong. I belong to a big, loving family."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L07-U1-LS1-V03","script":"roots. My roots are in a small village in Asir."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L07-U1-LS1-V04","script":"values. Honesty and generosity are family values."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L07-U1-LS1-V05","script":"personality. She has a cheerful personality."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L07-U1-LS1-V06","script":"influence. My grandfather had a strong influence on me."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L07-U1-LS1-V07","script":"upbringing. Her upbringing taught her to respect elders."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L07-U1-LS1-V08","script":"unique. Every fingerprint is unique."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L07-U1-LS1-V09","script":"generation. Three generations live in our house."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L07-U1-LS1-V10","script":"ancestor. My ancestors were traders on the incense route."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L07-U1-LS1-V11","script":"proud. I am proud of my country."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L07-U1-LS1-V12","script":"background. Students in our class come from different backgrounds."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L07-U1-LS1-V13","script":"faith. Faith gives many people strength."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L07-U1-LS1-V14","script":"custom. Serving coffee to guests is a Saudi custom."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L07-U1-LS1-V15","script":"reflect. I reflected on what my grandmother said."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L07-U1-LS1-V16","script":"inherit. She inherited her grandmother's dallah."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L07-U1-LS1-V17","script":"embrace. He learned to embrace his family's traditions."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L07-U1-LS1-V18","script":"transform. The trip transformed the way I see my roots."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L07-U1-LS1-V19","script":"perceive. I used to perceive old things as boring."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L07-U1-LS1-VID","script":"Who are you? Your name, your family, your faith, your language… What shapes who we are? Identity, belong, roots, values, personality, influence, upbringing, unique, generation, ancestor, proud, background, faith, custom! We inherit, we reflect, we embrace – and sometimes the way we perceive our roots is transformed! Old, antique, worn-out – the same meaning but different feelings! That's denotation and connotation. Choose your words carefully – they carry feelings to your reader!"}]},{"id":"RW-L07-U1-LS2","title":"My Grandmother's Dallah","lesson_type":"close_reading","objectives":["read a first-person narrative of about 580 words about a family object","analyze how the narrator's attitude is shown through word choice and connotation","explain how an object becomes a symbol and how its meaning grows during the story"],"objectives_ar":["قراءة سرد بضمير المتكلم من نحو 580 كلمة عن قطعة عائلية.","تحليل كيف يظهر موقف الراوية من خلال اختيار الكلمات وإيحاءاتها.","شرح كيف تصبح القطعة رمزًا وكيف يتسع معناها في أثناء القصة."],"success_criteria":["I can quote two phrases that show Lulwa's attitude at the beginning and two at the end.","I can explain what the dallah symbolizes with evidence.","I can explain the effect of the repeated phrase *as if it were made of glass*."],"success_criteria_ar":["أستطيع اقتباس عبارتين تُظهران موقف لولوة في البداية وعبارتين في النهاية.","أستطيع شرح ما ترمز إليه الدلّة بالدليل.","أستطيع شرح أثر تكرار عبارة «كأنها مصنوعة من زجاج»."],"map_area":"Literary Text: Key Ideas and Details","vocabulary":[{"word":"spout","pos":"noun","ipa":"/spaʊt/","definition":"the narrow tube through which liquid is poured from a pot","arabic":"صنبور / بلبلة الدلة","example":"The dallah has a long curved spout.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L07-U1-LS2-V01","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L07-U1-LS2-V01","tier":"core"},{"word":"dent","pos":"noun","ipa":"/dɛnt/","definition":"a small hollow made in a hard surface by hitting it","arabic":"انبعاج / طَعجة","example":"There is a small dent near the handle.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L07-U1-LS2-V02","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L07-U1-LS2-V02","tier":"core"},{"word":"roast","pos":"verb","ipa":"/roʊst/","definition":"to cook something such as coffee beans or meat with dry heat","arabic":"يحمّص / يشوي","example":"We roasted the beans lightly.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L07-U1-LS2-V03","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L07-U1-LS2-V03","tier":"core"},{"word":"settle","pos":"verb","ipa":"/ˈsɛtəl/","definition":"to sink slowly to the bottom of a liquid","arabic":"يترسّب / يستقر","example":"Wait for the grounds to settle.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L07-U1-LS2-V04","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L07-U1-LS2-V04","tier":"core"},{"word":"majlis","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈmædʒlɪs/","definition":"a sitting room where a family receives guests","arabic":"مجلس","example":"The majlis was full of relatives.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L07-U1-LS2-V05","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L07-U1-LS2-V05","tier":"core"},{"word":"sip","pos":"verb / noun","ipa":"/sɪp/","definition":"to drink slowly in very small amounts","arabic":"يرتشف / رشفة","example":"He sipped the coffee slowly.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L07-U1-LS2-V06","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L07-U1-LS2-V06","tier":"core"},{"word":"symbol","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈsɪmbəl/","definition":"an object that stands for a bigger idea","arabic":"رمز","example":"The dallah is a symbol of hospitality.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L07-U1-LS2-V07","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L07-U1-LS2-V07","tier":"core"}],"explanation":{"text":"In a **first-person narrative**, the narrator tells the story using **I** and **my**. We see everything through the narrator's eyes, so her **word choice** shows her **attitude** – her feelings and opinions. When the attitude changes, the **words** change too.\n\nWatch the **connotations**: at the beginning, Lulwa calls the dallah *just an old pot* and admires the *shiny electric coffee machine*. At the end, she calls it *a bridge* and says *every dent and every scratch told a story*. The same object, but warm, respectful words show her new attitude.\n\nA **symbol** is an object that stands for a **bigger idea**. Writers make an object a symbol by **returning to it** and giving it **meaning** through stories and feelings. The dallah becomes a symbol of **family history, hospitality and identity**. The **repeated phrase** *as if it were made of glass* shows how precious it is – first to Grandmother, and finally to Lulwa.","examples":["Beginning attitude: *I hardly noticed it … To me, it was just an old pot.* → careless, unimpressed.","Connotation: *shiny electric coffee machine* (modern, positive for her) vs. *just an old pot* (dismissive).","Turning point: *I looked at the dent differently after that.* → the dent becomes a memory, not a flaw.","Grandmother's attitude: *lifted the dallah … with both hands, as if it were made of glass* → great respect.","Sensory details: *the color of the desert at sunset … smelled sweet and sharp* → the narrator now notices and enjoys.","Symbol: *It was a bridge between my great-grandmother and me.* → the dallah = connection across generations.","Symbol: *Every dent and every scratch told a story about who we are.* → the dallah = family identity.","Ending attitude: *I lift the dallah down with both hands, as if it were made of glass.* → Lulwa now treats it as Grandmother did.","Balance: *We still have the coffee machine* → she keeps modern life AND tradition."],"common_errors":["✗ Thinking the narrator is the writer. → ✓ The narrator is the character telling the story (Lulwa). (Narrator ≠ author.)","✗ Saying a symbol is just a thing in the story. → ✓ Explain the bigger idea it stands for. (Object + idea.)","✗ Missing the repeated phrase. → ✓ Repetition often signals an important change. (Notice repetition.)","✗ Lulwa hates the coffee machine at the end. → ✓ She keeps using it but respects the dallah too. (Read carefully.)","✗ The dallah belong to her mother. → ✓ The dallah belonged to her mother. (Past simple → **belonged**.)","✗ Quoting without explaining. → ✓ Explain what the words show about her attitude. (Quote + explain.)","✗ Ignoring sensory details. → ✓ Sensory words show what the narrator now notices. (Look at senses.)"],"arabic":"في السرد بضمير المتكلم تروي الراوية القصة بـ «أنا» و«لي»، فنرى كل شيء بعينيها، ويُظهر اختيارها للكلمات موقفها ومشاعرها، وعندما يتغير الموقف تتغير الكلمات.\n\nلاحظ الإيحاءات: في البداية تسمّي لولوة الدلّة «مجرد إبريق قديم»، وفي النهاية تسميها «جسرًا» وتقول إن «كل انبعاج وخدش يحكي قصة». الشيء نفسه بكلمات دافئة تُظهر موقفها الجديد.\n\nالرمز شيء يدل على فكرة أكبر، ويجعله الكاتب رمزًا بالعودة إليه وإعطائه معنى من خلال القصص والمشاعر. والدلّة رمز لتاريخ العائلة والضيافة والهوية، وتكرار عبارة «كأنها مصنوعة من زجاج» يُظهر قيمتها.\n\nانتبه: الراوية شخصية في القصة وليست بالضرورة الكاتب."},"video":{"script":[{"time":"00:00–00:30","narration":"The dallah sat on the top shelf, and for most of my life I hardly noticed it…","on_screen":"My Grandmother's Dallah ☕","visual":"A brass dallah on a high kitchen shelf; a teenager scrolling on a phone below."},{"time":"00:30–01:40","narration":"This dallah belonged to my mother… Do you see this dent?","on_screen":"The story of the dent","visual":"Grandmother holds the dallah with both hands; a flashback of a young man dropping it in excitement."},{"time":"01:40–02:40","narration":"We roasted the beans… ground fresh cardamom… waited for the grounds to settle.","on_screen":"Patience and respect","visual":"Close-ups of roasting beans, cardamom, saffron threads, coffee pouring."},{"time":"02:40–03:30","narration":"It tastes exactly like your mother's…","on_screen":"Eid in the majlis","visual":"A girl serving coffee to a ninety-two-year-old great-uncle; tears in Grandmother's eyes."},{"time":"03:30–04:00","narration":"Just an old pot… a bridge between generations. Same object – new words, new meaning.","on_screen":"Attitude · Connotation · Symbol","visual":"The words 'just an old pot' transform into 'a bridge'."}],"captions_ar":["كانت الدلّة على الرف العلوي، ولمعظم حياتي لم أكد ألاحظها…","هذه الدلّة كانت لأمي… هل ترين هذا الانبعاج؟","حمّصنا البن… وطحنّا الهيل الطازج… وانتظرنا حتى يترسّب التفل.","طعمها تمامًا مثل قهوة أمك…","مجرد إبريق قديم… جسر بين الأجيال. الشيء نفسه بكلمات جديدة ومعنى جديد."],"voice":"Narrator: American English, warm teenage female voice for Lulwa; gentle older voice for Grandmother; speed 1.0."},"texts":[{"type":"reading","title":"My Grandmother's Dallah","body":"The dallah sat on the top shelf of my grandmother's kitchen, and for most of my life I hardly noticed it. It was brass, with a long curved spout like a bird's beak and a small dent near the handle. To me, it was just an old pot. At home, we had a shiny electric coffee machine that made coffee at the press of a button. Why would anyone need anything else?\n\nThat changed the winter I turned fourteen, when Grandmother's knees became too weak for her to stand at the stove for long. Eid was coming, and our whole family – more than thirty people – would gather at her house in Riyadh.\n\n\"Lulwa,\" she said one evening, \"this year, you will make the coffee.\"\n\nI laughed. \"Grandmother, I'll bring our machine. It's faster.\"\n\nShe did not laugh. She lifted the dallah down from the shelf with both hands, as if it were made of glass, and placed it on the table between us.\n\n\"This dallah belonged to my mother,\" she said. \"She carried it from our village in Najd when she married. It has poured coffee for weddings, for guests from Makkah, for neighbors who came to share good news – and bad news, too. Do you see this dent? Your grandfather dropped it the day your father was born. He was so excited that his hands were shaking.\"\n\nI looked at the dent differently after that. Grandmother told me more stories that night: about the summer the whole village gathered around one small radio to hear the news, and about a poet who once drank from this very dallah and praised its long, proud spout in a verse.\n\nFor the next week, Grandmother taught me. We roasted the beans lightly until they turned the color of the desert at sunset. We ground fresh cardamom until the whole kitchen smelled sweet and sharp. She showed me how to let the coffee boil gently, how to add a few threads of saffron and how to wait for the grounds to settle. \"Coffee is not about speed,\" she said. \"It is about patience – and respect.\"\n\nShe also taught me the customs. Hold the dallah in your left hand and the small cups in your right. Serve the oldest guest first. Pour only a little, and keep refilling the cup until the guest gently shakes it to say \"enough.\"\n\nOn the morning of Eid, my hands were shaking as much as my grandfather's must have been. The majlis was full of aunts, uncles and cousins, and everyone was watching. I started with my great-uncle, who was ninety-two. I poured a little coffee into his cup and handed it to him with my right hand.\n\nHe sipped slowly. Then he smiled and turned to my grandmother. \"It tastes exactly like your mother's,\" he said.\n\nMy grandmother's eyes filled with tears, and so did mine.\n\nThat day, I understood something I had never understood before. The dallah was not just an old pot. It was a bridge between my great-grandmother and me – between a village I had never seen and the city where I live. Every dent and every scratch told a story about who we are.\n\nNow the dallah sits on the top shelf of my own room. We still have the coffee machine, and I use it on busy school mornings. But when guests come, I lift the dallah down with both hands, as if it were made of glass.","word_count":576,"audio_id":"AUD-RW-L07-U1-LS2-T1","id":"TXT-RW-L07-U1-LS2-1","genre":"narrative","role":"main","difficulty":"800L–950L","glossary":[{"word":"dallah","definition":"a traditional Arabic coffee pot with a long curved spout","arabic":"دلّة"},{"word":"majlis","definition":"a sitting room where a family receives guests","arabic":"مجلس"},{"word":"grounds","definition":"the small pieces of coffee left at the bottom after making coffee","arabic":"تفل القهوة"}]}],"activities":[{"id":"RW-L07-U1-LS2-A1","type":"mcq","stage":"controlled","instructions":"Controlled practice: Read the story. Choose the answer.","instructions_ar":"اقرأ القصة واختر الإجابة.","items":[{"q":"Where did the dallah sit at the beginning of the story?","answer":"on the top shelf of Grandmother's kitchen","explanation":"Paragraph 1.","explanation_ar":"على الرف العلوي في المطبخ.","options":["on the table in the majlis","in Lulwa's school bag","on the top shelf of Grandmother's kitchen","next to the coffee machine"],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"Why did Lulwa have to make the coffee for Eid?","answer":"Grandmother's knees were too weak for her to stand at the stove for long.","explanation":"Paragraph 2.","explanation_ar":"ضعف ركبتي الجدة.","options":["Grandmother's knees were too weak for her to stand at the stove for long.","Her mother was traveling.","The coffee machine was broken.","She won a competition."],"dok":1,"skill":"cause and effect","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"Where did the dallah first come from?","answer":"a village in Najd","explanation":"Paragraph 6.","explanation_ar":"قرية في نجد.","options":["a shop in Jeddah","a market in Makkah","a village in Najd","a factory in Riyadh"],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"How did the dallah get its dent?","answer":"Grandfather dropped it the day Lulwa's father was born.","explanation":"Paragraph 6.","explanation_ar":"أسقطه الجد يوم وُلد والد لولوة.","options":["Lulwa dropped it at Eid.","Grandfather dropped it the day Lulwa's father was born.","It fell during a sandstorm.","A guest knocked it over."],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"Who did Lulwa serve first on Eid morning?","answer":"her ninety-two-year-old great-uncle","explanation":"Paragraph 10.","explanation_ar":"خال/عم والدها ذو الاثنين والتسعين عامًا.","options":["her ninety-two-year-old great-uncle","her grandmother","her youngest cousin","her father"],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"How does a guest show that he has had enough coffee?","answer":"He gently shakes the cup.","explanation":"Paragraph 9.","explanation_ar":"يهزّ الفنجان.","options":["He puts the cup upside down.","He says nothing and leaves.","He pours the coffee back.","He gently shakes the cup."],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"}]},{"id":"RW-L07-U1-LS2-A2","type":"mcq","stage":"guided","instructions":"Guided practice: Narrator, connotation and symbol. Choose.","instructions_ar":"الراوية والإيحاء والرمز: اختر.","items":[{"q":"What do the words *just an old pot* show about Lulwa at the beginning?","answer":"She does not value the dallah.","explanation":"Connotation.","explanation_ar":"لا تقدّر الدلّة.","options":["She loves the dallah deeply.","She does not value the dallah.","She is afraid of the dallah.","She wants to sell the dallah."],"dok":2,"skill":"connotation","stage":"guided"},{"q":"What does *She lifted the dallah … with both hands, as if it were made of glass* show about Grandmother?","answer":"She treats the dallah as very precious.","explanation":"Simile.","explanation_ar":"تعامل الدلّة كشيء ثمين.","options":["She treats the dallah as very precious.","The dallah is made of glass.","Her hands are weak.","She is angry with Lulwa."],"dok":2,"skill":"figurative language","stage":"guided"},{"q":"Which sentence marks the TURNING POINT in Lulwa's attitude?","answer":"I looked at the dent differently after that.","explanation":"Turning point.","explanation_ar":"نقطة التحول.","options":["Eid was coming.","We still have the coffee machine.","I looked at the dent differently after that.","He sipped slowly."],"dok":2,"skill":"character change","stage":"guided"},{"q":"What does the dallah SYMBOLIZE by the end of the story?","answer":"the connection between generations and the family's identity","explanation":"Symbol.","explanation_ar":"الصلة بين الأجيال وهوية العائلة.","options":["modern technology","the connection between generations and the family's identity","the price of coffee","Lulwa's school"],"dok":2,"skill":"symbolism","stage":"guided"},{"q":"Why does the author repeat *as if it were made of glass* at the end?","answer":"to show that Lulwa now treats the dallah with the same respect as Grandmother","explanation":"Repetition.","explanation_ar":"لإظهار أنها صارت تحترمها كجدتها.","options":["to show that Lulwa now treats the dallah with the same respect as Grandmother","to show that the dallah broke","to describe the weather","to make the story longer"],"dok":3,"skill":"author's craft","stage":"guided"},{"q":"Why does Lulwa still use the coffee machine on school mornings?","answer":"She keeps modern life and tradition together in a balanced way.","explanation":"Inference.","explanation_ar":"توازن بين الحديث والتقليدي.","options":["She still thinks the dallah is useless.","Her grandmother told her to.","The dallah is broken.","She keeps modern life and tradition together in a balanced way."],"dok":3,"skill":"inference","stage":"guided"}]},{"id":"RW-L07-U1-LS2-A3","type":"highlight","stage":"freer","instructions":"Find the evidence: Find the evidence.","instructions_ar":"ابحث عن الدليل.","items":[{"q":"Underline the sentence where Lulwa states what she finally understood about the dallah.","answer":"The dallah was not just an old pot.","explanation":"Realization.","explanation_ar":"الإدراك.","evidence":"The dallah was not just an old pot.","text_id":"TXT-RW-L07-U1-LS2-1","dok":2,"skill":"character change","stage":"freer","sentences":["The dallah sat on the top shelf of my grandmother's kitchen, and for most of my life I hardly noticed it.","It was brass, with a long curved spout like a bird's beak and a small dent near the handle.","To me, it was just an old pot.","At home, we had a shiny electric coffee machine that made coffee at the press of a button.","Why would anyone need anything else?","That changed the winter I turned fourteen, when Grandmother's knees became too weak for her to stand at the stove for long.","Eid was coming, and our whole family – more than thirty people – would gather at her house in Riyadh.","\"Lulwa,\" she said one evening, \"this year, you will make the coffee.\"","I laughed.","\"Grandmother, I'll bring our machine.","It's faster.\"","She did not laugh.","She lifted the dallah down from the shelf with both hands, as if it were made of glass, and placed it on the table between us.","\"This dallah belonged to my mother,\" she said.","\"She carried it from our village in Najd when she married.","It has poured coffee for weddings, for guests from Makkah, for neighbors who came to share good news – and bad news, too.","Do you see this dent?","Your grandfather dropped it the day your father was born.","He was so excited that his hands were shaking.\"","I looked at the dent differently after that.","Grandmother told me more stories that night: about the summer the whole village gathered around one small radio to hear the news, and about a poet who once drank from this very dallah and praised its long, proud spout in a verse.","For the next week, Grandmother taught me.","We roasted the beans lightly until they turned the color of the desert at sunset.","We ground fresh cardamom until the whole kitchen smelled sweet and sharp.","She showed me how to let the coffee boil gently, how to add a few threads of saffron and how to wait for the grounds to settle.","\"Coffee is not about speed,\" she said.","\"It is about patience – and respect.\"","She also taught me the customs.","Hold the dallah in your left hand and the small cups in your right.","Serve the oldest guest first.","Pour only a little, and keep refilling the cup until the guest gently shakes it to say \"enough.\"","On the morning of Eid, my hands were shaking as much as my grandfather's must have been.","The majlis was full of aunts, uncles and cousins, and everyone was watching.","I started with my great-uncle, who was ninety-two.","I poured a little coffee into his cup and handed it to him with my right hand.","He sipped slowly.","Then he smiled and turned to my grandmother.","\"It tastes exactly like your mother's,\" he said.","My grandmother's eyes filled with tears, and so did mine.","That day, I understood something I had never understood before.","The dallah was not just an old pot.","It was a bridge between my great-grandmother and me – between a village I had never seen and the city where I live.","Every dent and every scratch told a story about who we are.","Now the dallah sits on the top shelf of my own room.","We still have the coffee machine, and I use it on busy school mornings.","But when guests come, I lift the dallah down with both hands, as if it were made of glass."]},{"q":"Underline Grandmother's sentence that teaches a value, not just a recipe.","answer":"\"Coffee is not about speed,\" she said. \"It is about patience – and respect.\"","explanation":"Values.","explanation_ar":"القيم.","evidence":"\"Coffee is not about speed,\" she said. \"It is about patience – and respect.\"","text_id":"TXT-RW-L07-U1-LS2-1","dok":2,"skill":"theme","stage":"freer","sentences":["The dallah sat on the top shelf of my grandmother's kitchen, and for most of my life I hardly noticed it.","It was brass, with a long curved spout like a bird's beak and a small dent near the handle.","To me, it was just an old pot.","At home, we had a shiny electric coffee machine that made coffee at the press of a button.","Why would anyone need anything else?","That changed the winter I turned fourteen, when Grandmother's knees became too weak for her to stand at the stove for long.","Eid was coming, and our whole family – more than thirty people – would gather at her house in Riyadh.","\"Lulwa,\" she said one evening, \"this year, you will make the coffee.\"","I laughed.","\"Grandmother, I'll bring our machine.","It's faster.\"","She did not laugh.","She lifted the dallah down from the shelf with both hands, as if it were made of glass, and placed it on the table between us.","\"This dallah belonged to my mother,\" she said.","\"She carried it from our village in Najd when she married.","It has poured coffee for weddings, for guests from Makkah, for neighbors who came to share good news – and bad news, too.","Do you see this dent?","Your grandfather dropped it the day your father was born.","He was so excited that his hands were shaking.\"","I looked at the dent differently after that.","Grandmother told me more stories that night: about the summer the whole village gathered around one small radio to hear the news, and about a poet who once drank from this very dallah and praised its long, proud spout in a verse.","For the next week, Grandmother taught me.","We roasted the beans lightly until they turned the color of the desert at sunset.","We ground fresh cardamom until the whole kitchen smelled sweet and sharp.","She showed me how to let the coffee boil gently, how to add a few threads of saffron and how to wait for the grounds to settle.","\"Coffee is not about speed,\" she said.","\"It is about patience – and respect.\"","She also taught me the customs.","Hold the dallah in your left hand and the small cups in your right.","Serve the oldest guest first.","Pour only a little, and keep refilling the cup until the guest gently shakes it to say \"enough.\"","On the morning of Eid, my hands were shaking as much as my grandfather's must have been.","The majlis was full of aunts, uncles and cousins, and everyone was watching.","I started with my great-uncle, who was ninety-two.","I poured a little coffee into his cup and handed it to him with my right hand.","He sipped slowly.","Then he smiled and turned to my grandmother.","\"It tastes exactly like your mother's,\" he said.","My grandmother's eyes filled with tears, and so did mine.","That day, I understood something I had never understood before.","The dallah was not just an old pot.","It was a bridge between my great-grandmother and me – between a village I had never seen and the city where I live.","Every dent and every scratch told a story about who we are.","Now the dallah sits on the top shelf of my own room.","We still have the coffee machine, and I use it on busy school mornings.","But when guests come, I lift the dallah down with both hands, as if it were made of glass."]},{"q":"What is the effect of the sensory details *the color of the desert at sunset* and *smelled sweet and sharp*?","answer":"They show that Lulwa is now noticing and enjoying the tradition.","explanation":"Author's craft.","explanation_ar":"تُظهر أنها صارت تلاحظ وتستمتع.","options":["They tell us the time of day.","They describe the weather in Riyadh.","They show that the coffee was burned.","They show that Lulwa is now noticing and enjoying the tradition."],"dok":3,"skill":"author's craft","stage":"freer"},{"q":"Why does the great-uncle's comment make Grandmother cry?","answer":"It shows that her mother's tradition lives on through Lulwa.","explanation":"Inference.","explanation_ar":"لأن تقليد أمها استمر.","options":["The coffee was too hot.","He did not like the coffee.","It shows that her mother's tradition lives on through Lulwa.","She was tired."],"dok":3,"skill":"inference","stage":"freer"}]},{"id":"RW-L07-U1-LS2-A4","type":"open","stage":"production","instructions":"Writing: Analyze the narrative.","instructions_ar":"حلّل السرد.","items":[{"q":"Compare Lulwa's words about the dallah at the beginning and at the end. Use two quotations.","answer":"At the beginning she says it was 'just an old pot', but at the end she calls it 'a bridge between my great-grandmother and me'.","explanation":"Attitude.","explanation_ar":"الموقف.","dok":2,"skill":"connotation","stage":"production"},{"q":"Explain what the dent symbolizes.","answer":"The dent stands for a family memory – the joy of Grandfather on the day Lulwa's father was born – so it shows that even flaws can carry love and history.","explanation":"Symbol.","explanation_ar":"الرمز.","dok":3,"skill":"symbolism","stage":"production"},{"q":"Which customs did Grandmother teach Lulwa? Name three.","answer":"Hold the dallah in the left hand and the cups in the right; serve the oldest guest first; keep refilling until the guest shakes the cup.","explanation":"Customs.","explanation_ar":"العادات.","dok":2,"skill":"key details","stage":"production"},{"q":"What is the theme of the story? Support it with evidence.","answer":"Family traditions connect us to our roots and help us understand who we are – Lulwa realizes 'every dent and every scratch told a story about who we are'.","explanation":"Theme.","explanation_ar":"الفكرة العامة.","dok":3,"skill":"theme","stage":"production"}]},{"id":"RW-L07-U1-LS2-TR-1","type":"timed_reading","instructions":"Timed reading: Read \"My Grandmother's Dallah\" aloud or silently. Try to finish in the time.","instructions_ar":"اقرأ النص وحاول أن تنهيه في الوقت المحدد.","items":[{"q":"Read “My Grandmother's Dallah”.","answer":"576 words","title":"My Grandmother's Dallah","body":"The dallah sat on the top shelf of my grandmother's kitchen, and for most of my life I hardly noticed it. It was brass, with a long curved spout like a bird's beak and a small dent near the handle. To me, it was just an old pot. At home, we had a shiny electric coffee machine that made coffee at the press of a button. Why would anyone need anything else?\n\nThat changed the winter I turned fourteen, when Grandmother's knees became too weak for her to stand at the stove for long. Eid was coming, and our whole family – more than thirty people – would gather at her house in Riyadh.\n\n\"Lulwa,\" she said one evening, \"this year, you will make the coffee.\"\n\nI laughed. \"Grandmother, I'll bring our machine. It's faster.\"\n\nShe did not laugh. She lifted the dallah down from the shelf with both hands, as if it were made of glass, and placed it on the table between us.\n\n\"This dallah belonged to my mother,\" she said. \"She carried it from our village in Najd when she married. It has poured coffee for weddings, for guests from Makkah, for neighbors who came to share good news – and bad news, too. Do you see this dent? Your grandfather dropped it the day your father was born. He was so excited that his hands were shaking.\"\n\nI looked at the dent differently after that. Grandmother told me more stories that night: about the summer the whole village gathered around one small radio to hear the news, and about a poet who once drank from this very dallah and praised its long, proud spout in a verse.\n\nFor the next week, Grandmother taught me. We roasted the beans lightly until they turned the color of the desert at sunset. We ground fresh cardamom until the whole kitchen smelled sweet and sharp. She showed me how to let the coffee boil gently, how to add a few threads of saffron and how to wait for the grounds to settle. \"Coffee is not about speed,\" she said. \"It is about patience – and respect.\"\n\nShe also taught me the customs. Hold the dallah in your left hand and the small cups in your right. Serve the oldest guest first. Pour only a little, and keep refilling the cup until the guest gently shakes it to say \"enough.\"\n\nOn the morning of Eid, my hands were shaking as much as my grandfather's must have been. The majlis was full of aunts, uncles and cousins, and everyone was watching. I started with my great-uncle, who was ninety-two. I poured a little coffee into his cup and handed it to him with my right hand.\n\nHe sipped slowly. Then he smiled and turned to my grandmother. \"It tastes exactly like your mother's,\" he said.\n\nMy grandmother's eyes filled with tears, and so did mine.\n\nThat day, I understood something I had never understood before. The dallah was not just an old pot. It was a bridge between my great-grandmother and me – between a village I had never seen and the city where I live. Every dent and every scratch told a story about who we are.\n\nNow the dallah sits on the top shelf of my own room. We still have the coffee machine, and I use it on busy school mornings. But when guests come, I lift the dallah down with both hands, as if it were made of glass.","words":576,"limit":280}],"time_limit_sec":280},{"id":"RW-L07-U1-LS2-ORG","type":"organizer","organizer":"Symbol Tracker","instructions":"Organizer: Track how Lulwa's words and the meaning of the dallah change. Add a quotation for each box.","items":[{"q":"Beginning: Lulwa's attitude + words","answer":"unimpressed: 'I hardly noticed it … just an old pot'; she prefers the 'shiny electric coffee machine'"},{"q":"What Grandmother tells her (the history)","answer":"It belonged to her great-grandmother, came from a village in Najd and served guests for decades."},{"q":"The dent: from flaw to memory","answer":"'Your grandfather dropped it the day your father was born' → 'I looked at the dent differently after that.'"},{"q":"The Eid moment","answer":"Her great-uncle says, 'It tastes exactly like your mother's.' Grandmother and Lulwa cry."},{"q":"What the dallah symbolizes","answer":"a bridge between generations; family history, hospitality and identity"},{"q":"Ending: Lulwa's attitude + words","answer":"respectful and proud: 'I lift the dallah down with both hands, as if it were made of glass.'"}]}],"game":{"name":"Symbol Detectives","mechanic":"timed_quiz","rules":["On this screen: answer the lesson questions as fast as you can.","In class: Symbol match: the child drags each object to its bigger idea and then chooses the quotation that proves it. Correct matches fill a family tree with leaves.","Teams draw an object card.","They choose the idea card it symbolizes.","They find the quotation that supports their choice.","Other teams may challenge with a different idea and better evidence.","Correct idea = 1 point; strong evidence = 2 points."],"data":[]},"exit_quiz":[{"q":"What is a *first-person narrator*?","answer":"a character who tells the story using I and my","explanation":"Definition.","explanation_ar":"راوٍ بضمير المتكلم.","options":["the person who drew the pictures","a character who tells the story using I and my","a narrator who knows everyone's thoughts","the reader"],"dok":1,"skill":"point of view"},{"q":"What is a *symbol*?","answer":"an object that stands for a bigger idea","explanation":"Definition.","explanation_ar":"رمز.","options":["an object that stands for a bigger idea","a kind of punctuation","a long word","the title of a story"],"dok":1,"skill":"symbolism"},{"q":"*Wait for the grounds to **settle**.* *Settle* means ___.","answer":"sink slowly to the bottom","explanation":"Context.","explanation_ar":"يترسّب.","options":["boil over","float to the top","sink slowly to the bottom","turn black"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary in context"},{"q":"What is a *majlis*?","answer":"a sitting room where a family receives guests","explanation":"Vocabulary.","explanation_ar":"مجلس.","options":["a coffee machine","a kitchen shelf","a sitting room where a family receives guests","a type of cup"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary"},{"q":"Which sentence about the dallah is correct?","answer":"The dallah belonged to Lulwa's great-grandmother.","explanation":"Past simple.","explanation_ar":"صحيحة.","options":["The dallah belong to Lulwa's great-grandmother.","The dallah belonged to Lulwa's great-grandmother.","The dallah belonging to Lulwa's great-grandmother.","The dallah was belong to Lulwa's great-grandmother."],"dok":2,"skill":"grammar"},{"q":"Which phrase shows Lulwa's attitude at the BEGINNING of the story?","answer":"I hardly noticed it","explanation":"Attitude.","explanation_ar":"في البداية.","options":["a bridge between my great-grandmother and me","as if it were made of glass","I hardly noticed it","every dent and every scratch told a story"],"dok":2,"skill":"connotation"},{"q":"What does Grandmother mean by *Coffee is not about speed*?","answer":"Making coffee well needs patience and respect, not hurry.","explanation":"Inference.","explanation_ar":"تحتاج إلى صبر واحترام.","options":["Making coffee well needs patience and respect, not hurry.","Coffee should be drunk quickly.","The machine is faster.","Coffee is not important."],"dok":2,"skill":"inference"},{"q":"Why does the author include Lulwa's shaking hands on Eid morning?","answer":"to connect her to her grandfather and show how important the moment is","explanation":"Author's craft.","explanation_ar":"لربطها بجدها وإظهار أهمية اللحظة.","options":["to show she was cold","to show she was sick","to explain why she dropped the dallah","to connect her to her grandfather and show how important the moment is"],"dok":2,"skill":"author's craft"},{"q":"How has the dallah's meaning CHANGED for Lulwa by the end?","answer":"It has changed from an old pot into a precious link to her family's past.","explanation":"Symbol + change.","explanation_ar":"من إبريق قديم إلى صلة ثمينة.","options":["It has changed from precious to useless.","It has changed from an old pot into a precious link to her family's past.","It has become a modern machine.","It has not changed at all."],"dok":3,"skill":"character change"},{"q":"Which statement BEST expresses the theme of *My Grandmother's Dallah*?","answer":"Family traditions connect generations and help us understand who we are.","explanation":"Theme.","explanation_ar":"التقاليد تربط الأجيال وتكشف هويتنا.","options":["New things are always better than old things.","Coffee should be made quickly.","Grandparents should not cook.","Family traditions connect generations and help us understand who we are."],"dok":3,"skill":"theme"}],"images":[{"id":"IMG-RW-L07-U1-LS2-V01","prompt":"Picture for the word 'spout': a dallah with its curved spout highlighted"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L07-U1-LS2-V02","prompt":"Picture for the word 'dent': a small dent in a brass pot"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L07-U1-LS2-V03","prompt":"Picture for the word 'roast': coffee beans roasting in a pan"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L07-U1-LS2-V04","prompt":"Picture for the word 'settle': coffee grounds at the bottom of a pot"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L07-U1-LS2-V05","prompt":"Picture for the word 'majlis': a traditional room with floor cushions"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L07-U1-LS2-V06","prompt":"Picture for the word 'sip': an old man sipping from a small cup"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L07-U1-LS2-V07","prompt":"Picture for the word 'symbol': a dallah with a heart and a family tree behind it"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L07-U1-LS2-01","prompt":"An old brass dallah above a modern coffee machine."},{"id":"IMG-RW-L07-U1-LS2-02","prompt":"A grandmother teaching her granddaughter to make Arabic coffee."},{"id":"IMG-RW-L07-U1-LS2-03","prompt":"Serving Arabic coffee to an elder on Eid."}],"audio":[{"id":"AUD-RW-L07-U1-LS2-V01","script":"spout. The dallah has a long curved spout."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L07-U1-LS2-V02","script":"dent. There is a small dent near the handle."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L07-U1-LS2-V03","script":"roast. We roasted the beans lightly."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L07-U1-LS2-V04","script":"settle. Wait for the grounds to settle."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L07-U1-LS2-V05","script":"majlis. The majlis was full of relatives."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L07-U1-LS2-V06","script":"sip. He sipped the coffee slowly."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L07-U1-LS2-V07","script":"symbol. The dallah is a symbol of hospitality."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L07-U1-LS2-T1","script":"The dallah sat on the top shelf of my grandmother's kitchen, and for most of my life I hardly noticed it. It was brass, with a long curved spout like a bird's beak and a small dent near the handle. To me, it was just an old pot. At home, we had a shiny electric coffee machine that made coffee at the press of a button. Why would anyone need anything else? That changed the winter I turned fourteen, when Grandmother's knees became too weak for her to stand at the stove for long. Eid was coming, and our whole family – more than thirty people – would gather at her house in Riyadh. \"Lulwa,\" she said one evening, \"this year, you will make the coffee.\" I laughed. \"Grandmother, I'll bring our machine. It's faster.\" She did not laugh. She lifted the dallah down from the shelf with both hands, as if it were made of glass, and placed it on the table between us. \"This dallah belonged to my mother,\" she said. \"She carried it from our village in Najd when she married. It has poured coffee for weddings, for guests from Makkah, for neighbors who came to share good news – and bad news, too. Do you see this dent? Your grandfather dropped it the day your father was born. He was so excited that his hands were shaking.\" I looked at the dent differently after that. Grandmother told me more stories that night: about the summer the whole village gathered around one small radio to hear the news, and about a poet who once drank from this very dallah and praised its long, proud spout in a verse. For the next week, Grandmother taught me. We roasted the beans lightly until they turned the color of the desert at sunset. We ground fresh cardamom until the whole kitchen smelled sweet and sharp. She showed me how to let the coffee boil gently, how to add a few threads of saffron and how to wait for the grounds to settle. \"Coffee is not about speed,\" she said. \"It is about patience – and respect.\" She also taught me the customs. Hold the dallah in your left hand and the small cups in your right. Serve the oldest guest first. Pour only a little, and keep refilling the cup until the guest gently shakes it to say \"enough.\" On the morning of Eid, my hands were shaking as much as my grandfather's must have been. The majlis was full of aunts, uncles and cousins, and everyone was watching. I started with my great-uncle, who was ninety-two. I poured a little coffee into his cup and handed it to him with my right hand. He sipped slowly. Then he smiled and turned to my grandmother. \"It tastes exactly like your mother's,\" he said. My grandmother's eyes filled with tears, and so did mine. That day, I understood something I had never understood before. The dallah was not just an old pot. It was a bridge between my great-grandmother and me – between a village I had never seen and the city where I live. Every dent and every scratch told a story about who we are. Now the dallah sits on the top shelf of my own room. We still have the coffee machine, and I use it on busy school mornings. But when guests come, I lift the dallah down with both hands, as if it were made of glass."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L07-U1-LS2-VID","script":"The dallah sat on the top shelf, and for most of my life I hardly noticed it… This dallah belonged to my mother… Do you see this dent? We roasted the beans… ground fresh cardamom… waited for the grounds to settle. It tastes exactly like your mother's… Just an old pot… a bridge between generations. Same object – new words, new meaning."}]},{"id":"RW-L07-U1-LS3","title":"Theme and character change: \"Roots\"","lesson_type":"strategy_paired_text","objectives":["trace how a character or speaker changes: beginning attitude → turning point → new attitude","explain how a character's change reveals the theme of a story and a poem","compare the changes and themes in *My Grandmother's Dallah* and *Roots*"],"objectives_ar":["تتبّع تغيّر الشخصية أو المتكلم: الموقف الأول ← نقطة التحول ← الموقف الجديد.","شرح كيف يكشف تغيّر الشخصية الفكرة العامة في قصة وقصيدة.","مقارنة التغيّرات والأفكار العامة في «دلّة جدتي» و«جذور»."],"success_criteria":["I can describe the speaker's attitude at the beginning and the end of the poem with quotations.","I can name the turning point in each text and explain what caused the change.","I can write a shared theme and explain how each text develops it."],"success_criteria_ar":["أستطيع وصف موقف المتكلم في بداية القصيدة ونهايتها بالاقتباسات.","أستطيع تحديد نقطة التحول في كل نص وشرح سببها.","أستطيع كتابة فكرة عامة مشتركة وشرح كيف يطوّرها كل نص."],"map_area":"Literary Text: Key Ideas and Details","vocabulary":[{"word":"wadi","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈwɑdi/","definition":"a valley in the desert that is dry except when it rains","arabic":"وادٍ","example":"The call to prayer drifted over the wadi.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L07-U1-LS3-V01","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L07-U1-LS3-V01","tier":"core"},{"word":"bark","pos":"noun","ipa":"/bɑrk/","definition":"the hard outer covering of a tree","arabic":"لحاء الشجر","example":"I touched the rough bark of the palm.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L07-U1-LS3-V02","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L07-U1-LS3-V02","tier":"core"},{"word":"unroll","pos":"verb","ipa":"/ʌnˈroʊl/","definition":"to open something that was rolled up","arabic":"يفرد / ينبسط","example":"The road unrolled like a gray ribbon.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L07-U1-LS3-V03","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L07-U1-LS3-V03","tier":"core"},{"word":"drift","pos":"verb","ipa":"/drɪft/","definition":"to move slowly through the air or on water","arabic":"ينساب / ينجرف","example":"The sound drifted over the village.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L07-U1-LS3-V04","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L07-U1-LS3-V04","tier":"core"},{"word":"speaker","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈspikər/","definition":"the voice that talks in a poem","arabic":"المتكلم (في القصيدة)","example":"The speaker of the poem is a teenager.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L07-U1-LS3-V05","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L07-U1-LS3-V05","tier":"core"},{"word":"turning point","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈtɝnɪŋ ˌpɔɪnt/","definition":"the moment when a character begins to change","arabic":"نقطة التحول","example":"The visit to the village is the turning point.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L07-U1-LS3-V06","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L07-U1-LS3-V06","tier":"core"},{"word":"ashamed","pos":"adjective","ipa":"/əˈʃeɪmd/","definition":"feeling bad or embarrassed about something","arabic":"خجل / خَزيان","example":"At first, the speaker was ashamed of his roots.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L07-U1-LS3-V07","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L07-U1-LS3-V07","tier":"core"}],"explanation":{"text":"In many stories and poems, the main character (or the **speaker** of a poem) **changes**. To trace the change, find: (1) the **beginning attitude** – how the character thinks or feels; (2) the **turning point** – the event that causes the change; (3) the **new attitude** at the end. Support each part with a **quotation**.\n\nA character's change often **reveals the theme**. Ask: *What did the character learn?* Then turn it into a lesson for everyone. In *Roots*, the speaker learns to be proud of his origins → theme: ***Knowing and accepting our roots gives us strength.***\n\nTwo texts can share a **similar theme** but develop it differently. Compare: **who** changes, **what** causes the change, **how** the writer shows it (a story uses events and dialogue; a poem uses **images**, **figurative language** and **repetition**) and **what** each character learns.","examples":["Poem – beginning: *I folded my village into a small box / and pushed it under my bed.* → hiding his roots.","Poem – beginning: *I said my family name quietly, / as if it were a secret.* → ashamed.","Poem – turning point: the summer visit; Grandfather says *it never forgets / where it came from.*","Poem – change: *felt something open inside me, / like a door / I had kept closed.*","Poem – end: *I answer slowly – and with pride.* → proud of his roots.","Story – beginning: *To me, it was just an old pot.* → Story – end: *a bridge between my great-grandmother and me.*","Story – turning point: Grandmother's story of the dent and the Eid coffee.","Shared theme: ***Understanding where we come from helps us know who we are and gives us strength.***","Different methods: the story shows change through events and dialogue; the poem through the box image, the palm metaphor and the final image of the tree."],"common_errors":["✗ Describing the character only at the end. → ✓ Describe the beginning AND the end. (Before and after.)","✗ Naming the change without the cause. → ✓ Explain the turning point that caused it. (What made it change?)","✗ The theme is: roots. → ✓ The theme is: Knowing our roots gives us strength. (Theme = sentence.)","✗ The speaker is the poet. → ✓ The speaker is the voice in the poem. (Speaker ≠ poet.)","✗ He was ashamed from his family name. → ✓ He was ashamed of his family name. (ashamed **of**.)","✗ Saying the two texts are the same. → ✓ Explain the different methods each writer uses. (Same theme, different ways.)","✗ Quoting a whole stanza. → ✓ Quote the key words only. (Short quotations.)"],"arabic":"في كثير من القصص والقصائد تتغير الشخصية الرئيسية أو المتكلم. ولتتبّع التغيّر حدّد: الموقف الأول، ثم نقطة التحول (الحدث المسبّب)، ثم الموقف الجديد في النهاية، وادعم كلًّا منها باقتباس.\n\nتغيّر الشخصية يكشف غالبًا الفكرة العامة؛ فاسأل: ماذا تعلّمت الشخصية؟ ثم حوّله إلى درس للجميع. في «جذور» يتعلم المتكلم الاعتزاز بأصله، فالفكرة: معرفة جذورنا وتقبّلها يمنحنا القوة.\n\nقد يشترك نصان في فكرة متقاربة لكن يطوّرانها بطرق مختلفة: القصة بالأحداث والحوار، والقصيدة بالصور والمجاز والتكرار.\n\nانتبه: المتكلم في القصيدة صوت فيها وليس بالضرورة الشاعر، ونقول ashamed of."},"video":{"script":[{"time":"00:00–00:30","narration":"When we moved to the city, I folded my village into a small box…","on_screen":"Roots 🌴","visual":"A boy pushes a cardboard box under his bed in a city apartment."},{"time":"00:30–01:40","narration":"At night, the box under my bed was never quite silent…","on_screen":"The hidden village","visual":"Sounds rise from the box: palm leaves, goats, the call to prayer over a wadi."},{"time":"01:40–02:40","narration":"The roots go deeper than you think… it never forgets where it came from.","on_screen":"Turning point","visual":"Grandfather kneels by a date palm; roots glow beneath the sand."},{"time":"02:40–03:30","narration":"Ashamed → turning point → proud. Lulwa: just an old pot → a bridge. Two changes, one big idea!","on_screen":"Change reveals theme","visual":"Two timelines side by side: the poem and the story."},{"time":"03:30–04:00","narration":"A tree only stands tall when it holds on to the earth that holds it.","on_screen":"Theme","visual":"A tall palm in the wind, roots holding firm."}],"captions_ar":["عندما انتقلنا إلى المدينة، طويتُ قريتي في صندوق صغير…","في الليل لم يكن الصندوق تحت سريري صامتًا تمامًا…","الجذور أعمق مما تظن… فهي لا تنسى أبدًا من أين جاءت.","خجل ← نقطة تحول ← فخر. لولوة: مجرد إبريق ← جسر. تغيّران وفكرة كبيرة واحدة!","لا تقف الشجرة شامخة إلا إذا تمسّكت بالأرض التي تحتضنها."],"voice":"Narrator: American English, calm teenage male voice for the poem; clear teacher voice for the explanation; speed 1.0."},"texts":[{"type":"reading","title":"Roots","body":"When we moved to the city, / I folded my village into a small box / and pushed it under my bed. / I wanted new shoes, new words, / a voice that sounded like everyone else's.\n\nAt school, I laughed when they laughed / at the way my grandfather spoke, / and I said my family name quietly, / as if it were a secret / I was tired of keeping.\n\nI learned the names of shops and streets, / the songs on everyone's phones, / the fastest way to end a conversation / before anyone could ask / where I was really from.\n\nBut at night, the box under my bed / was never quite silent. / I heard the palm leaves whispering, / my grandmother calling the goats home, / the call to prayer drifting over the wadi.\n\nOne summer, my father drove us back. / The road unrolled like a long gray ribbon, / and the city shrank behind us / until it was / a glow in the mirror.\n\nThe old house was smaller than I remembered, / its mud walls cracked like an old map. / But my grandfather stood at the door / with open arms, / exactly as tall as my memories.\n\nHe took me to the date palm / his father had planted / the year he was born. / \"Look,\" he said, kneeling in the sand, / \"the roots go deeper than you think.\"\n\n\"In a storm, the palm bends,\" he said, / \"but it does not fall. / Do you know why? / Because it never forgets / where it came from.\"\n\nI touched the rough bark / and felt something open inside me, / like a door / I had kept closed / for too long.\n\nThat night, I unpacked the box. / I found my grandmother's songs, / the smell of bread on a clay stove, / and my family name, / shining like new gold.\n\nNow, in the city, I speak / my grandfather's Arabic and my classmates' English. / I wear new shoes on old paths, / and when someone asks where I am from, / I answer slowly – and with pride.\n\nA tree can grow toward any sky / and drink from any rain, / but it only stands tall / when it holds on to the earth / that holds it.","word_count":350,"audio_id":"AUD-RW-L07-U1-LS3-T1","id":"TXT-RW-L07-U1-LS3-1","genre":"poetry (original)","role":"paired","difficulty":"800L–950L","glossary":[{"word":"wadi","definition":"a valley in the desert that is dry except when it rains","arabic":"وادٍ"},{"word":"bark","definition":"the hard outer covering of a tree","arabic":"لحاء"},{"word":"unroll","definition":"to open something that was rolled up","arabic":"يفرد / ينبسط"}]}],"activities":[{"id":"RW-L07-U1-LS3-A1","type":"mcq","stage":"controlled","instructions":"Controlled practice: Read *Roots*. Choose the answer.","instructions_ar":"اقرأ القصيدة واختر الإجابة.","items":[{"q":"What did the speaker do with his village when the family moved to the city?","answer":"He folded it into a small box and pushed it under his bed.","explanation":"Stanza 1.","explanation_ar":"طواها في صندوق تحت السرير.","options":["He folded it into a small box and pushed it under his bed.","He wrote a book about it.","He visited it every weekend.","He forgot it completely."],"dok":1,"skill":"figurative language","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"How did the speaker say his family name at school?","answer":"quietly, as if it were a secret","explanation":"Stanza 2.","explanation_ar":"بصوت خافت كأنه سر.","options":["quietly, as if it were a secret","loudly and proudly","in English only","he never said it"],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"What sounds did the speaker hear at night?","answer":"palm leaves, his grandmother calling the goats and the call to prayer","explanation":"Stanza 4.","explanation_ar":"حفيف النخل ونداء الجدة والأذان.","options":["city traffic and phones","the school bell","music from the shops","palm leaves, his grandmother calling the goats and the call to prayer"],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"Who planted the date palm that Grandfather showed the speaker?","answer":"Grandfather's father","explanation":"Stanza 7.","explanation_ar":"والد الجد.","options":["the speaker's father","the speaker","a neighbor","Grandfather's father"],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"What did the speaker find when he unpacked the box?","answer":"his grandmother's songs, the smell of bread and his family name","explanation":"Stanza 10.","explanation_ar":"أغاني الجدة ورائحة الخبز واسم العائلة.","options":["new shoes and phones","school books","his grandmother's songs, the smell of bread and his family name","nothing at all"],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"How many stanzas does *Roots* have?","answer":"twelve","explanation":"Count.","explanation_ar":"اثنا عشر.","options":["ten","twelve","eight","fourteen"],"dok":1,"skill":"poem structure","stage":"controlled"}]},{"id":"RW-L07-U1-LS3-A2","type":"mcq","stage":"guided","instructions":"Guided practice: Theme and character change. Choose.","instructions_ar":"الفكرة العامة وتغيّر الشخصية: اختر.","items":[{"q":"How does the speaker of *Roots* feel about his roots at the BEGINNING?","answer":"ashamed and eager to hide them","explanation":"Beginning.","explanation_ar":"خجل ويريد إخفاءها.","options":["proud and happy","ashamed and eager to hide them","angry with his grandfather","bored but curious"],"dok":2,"skill":"character change","stage":"guided"},{"q":"What is the TURNING POINT for the speaker of *Roots*?","answer":"the summer visit when Grandfather shows him the palm","explanation":"Turning point.","explanation_ar":"زيارة الصيف وحديث الجد عند النخلة.","options":["the first day at the city school","learning songs on his phone","the summer visit when Grandfather shows him the palm","buying new shoes"],"dok":2,"skill":"character change","stage":"guided"},{"q":"What does *it never forgets / where it came from* suggest about people?","answer":"People stay strong when they remember their roots.","explanation":"Figurative meaning.","explanation_ar":"الناس يقوون بتذكّر جذورهم.","options":["Palm trees have memories.","Storms are dangerous.","People stay strong when they remember their roots.","People should never move."],"dok":2,"skill":"figurative language","stage":"guided"},{"q":"Which line shows the speaker's NEW attitude?","answer":"I answer slowly – and with pride.","explanation":"End.","explanation_ar":"الموقف الجديد.","options":["I folded my village into a small box","I laughed when they laughed","the fastest way to end a conversation","I answer slowly – and with pride."],"dok":2,"skill":"character change","stage":"guided"},{"q":"Which SHARED theme fits both *Roots* and *My Grandmother's Dallah*?","answer":"Understanding where we come from helps us know who we are.","explanation":"Shared theme.","explanation_ar":"الفكرة المشتركة.","options":["City life is better than village life.","Coffee machines are faster.","Old houses are always small.","Understanding where we come from helps us know who we are."],"dok":3,"skill":"theme","stage":"guided"},{"q":"How does the poem show the change DIFFERENTLY from the story?","answer":"It uses images like the box, the palm and the door instead of a sequence of events and dialogue.","explanation":"Genre.","explanation_ar":"بالصور الشعرية.","options":["It uses images like the box, the palm and the door instead of a sequence of events and dialogue.","It uses more dialogue than the story.","It has headings and facts.","It does not show any change."],"dok":3,"skill":"comparing texts","stage":"guided"}]},{"id":"RW-L07-U1-LS3-A3","type":"organizer","stage":"freer","instructions":"Organizer: Complete the Change and Theme Map.","instructions_ar":"أكمل خريطة التغيّر والفكرة العامة.","items":[{"q":"Poem: write the speaker's beginning attitude with a quotation.","answer":"He is ashamed and hides his roots: 'I said my family name quietly, / as if it were a secret.'","explanation":"Beginning.","explanation_ar":"البداية.","dok":2,"skill":"character change","stage":"freer"},{"q":"Poem: write the turning point and its cause.","answer":"The summer visit to the village; Grandfather shows him the palm and says it 'never forgets where it came from', and the speaker feels 'something open inside' him.","explanation":"Turning point.","explanation_ar":"نقطة التحول.","dok":2,"skill":"character change","stage":"freer"},{"q":"Poem: write the new attitude with a quotation.","answer":"He is proud of his roots: 'my family name, / shining like new gold' and 'I answer slowly – and with pride.'","explanation":"End.","explanation_ar":"النهاية.","dok":2,"skill":"character change","stage":"freer"},{"q":"Write the shared theme and explain how each text develops it.","answer":"Understanding our roots gives us strength. The story shows it through Lulwa's lessons and the Eid coffee; the poem shows it through images of a hidden box and a strong palm tree.","explanation":"Theme.","explanation_ar":"الفكرة العامة.","dok":3,"skill":"theme","stage":"freer"}]},{"id":"RW-L07-U1-LS3-A4","type":"open","stage":"production","instructions":"Writing: Interpret and compare.","instructions_ar":"فسّر وقارن.","items":[{"q":"Explain the metaphor of the box in stanzas 1 and 10.","answer":"The box stands for the speaker's hidden identity: he hides his village and family name in it when he is ashamed, and he 'unpacks' them when he becomes proud.","explanation":"Metaphor.","explanation_ar":"الاستعارة.","dok":3,"skill":"figurative language","stage":"production"},{"q":"Explain the final stanza in your own words.","answer":"People can grow and live anywhere, but they are strongest when they stay connected to the family and culture that made them.","explanation":"Interpret.","explanation_ar":"فسّر.","dok":3,"skill":"theme","stage":"production"},{"q":"Compare Lulwa and the speaker: how are their changes alike and different?","answer":"Both learn to value their family's past through a grandparent. Lulwa changes through cooking and serving coffee, while the speaker changes during a visit to his village and a talk under a palm tree.","explanation":"Compare.","explanation_ar":"قارن.","dok":3,"skill":"comparing texts","stage":"production"},{"q":"Have you ever felt shy about part of your identity? What helped you feel proud of it?","answer":"I used to feel shy about my accent, but when my teacher asked me to teach the class some words from my region, I felt proud.","explanation":"Connect.","explanation_ar":"ربط.","dok":2,"skill":"connect","stage":"production"}]},{"id":"RW-L07-U1-LS3-TR-1","type":"timed_reading","instructions":"Timed reading: Read \"Roots\" aloud or silently. Try to finish in the time.","instructions_ar":"اقرأ النص وحاول أن تنهيه في الوقت المحدد.","items":[{"q":"Read “Roots”.","answer":"350 words","title":"Roots","body":"When we moved to the city, / I folded my village into a small box / and pushed it under my bed. / I wanted new shoes, new words, / a voice that sounded like everyone else's.\n\nAt school, I laughed when they laughed / at the way my grandfather spoke, / and I said my family name quietly, / as if it were a secret / I was tired of keeping.\n\nI learned the names of shops and streets, / the songs on everyone's phones, / the fastest way to end a conversation / before anyone could ask / where I was really from.\n\nBut at night, the box under my bed / was never quite silent. / I heard the palm leaves whispering, / my grandmother calling the goats home, / the call to prayer drifting over the wadi.\n\nOne summer, my father drove us back. / The road unrolled like a long gray ribbon, / and the city shrank behind us / until it was / a glow in the mirror.\n\nThe old house was smaller than I remembered, / its mud walls cracked like an old map. / But my grandfather stood at the door / with open arms, / exactly as tall as my memories.\n\nHe took me to the date palm / his father had planted / the year he was born. / \"Look,\" he said, kneeling in the sand, / \"the roots go deeper than you think.\"\n\n\"In a storm, the palm bends,\" he said, / \"but it does not fall. / Do you know why? / Because it never forgets / where it came from.\"\n\nI touched the rough bark / and felt something open inside me, / like a door / I had kept closed / for too long.\n\nThat night, I unpacked the box. / I found my grandmother's songs, / the smell of bread on a clay stove, / and my family name, / shining like new gold.\n\nNow, in the city, I speak / my grandfather's Arabic and my classmates' English. / I wear new shoes on old paths, / and when someone asks where I am from, / I answer slowly – and with pride.\n\nA tree can grow toward any sky / and drink from any rain, / but it only stands tall / when it holds on to the earth / that holds it.","words":350,"limit":176}],"time_limit_sec":176},{"id":"RW-L07-U1-LS3-ORG","type":"organizer","organizer":"Change and Theme Map","instructions":"Organizer: Trace the change in each text with quotations, then write the shared theme.","items":[{"q":"Poem – beginning attitude + quotation","answer":"ashamed, hiding his roots: 'I folded my village into a small box' / 'I said my family name quietly'"},{"q":"Poem – turning point + cause","answer":"the summer visit; Grandfather at the palm: 'it never forgets where it came from'"},{"q":"Poem – new attitude + quotation","answer":"proud: 'my family name, shining like new gold' / 'I answer slowly – and with pride'"},{"q":"Story – beginning, turning point, end (Lulwa)","answer":"'just an old pot' → Grandmother's stories and Eid coffee → 'a bridge between my great-grandmother and me'"},{"q":"Shared theme (one sentence)","answer":"Understanding where we come from helps us know who we are and gives us strength."},{"q":"How each writer shows the change differently","answer":"The story uses events and dialogue; the poem uses images (the box, the palm, the door) and a final metaphor of the tree."}]}],"game":{"name":"Before, Turn, After","mechanic":"timed_quiz","rules":["On this screen: answer the lesson questions as fast as you can.","In class: Change path: the child places quotation stones on a path from BEFORE to AFTER, passing through a TURN gate; then chooses the theme card the path leads to. A correct path lights up a palm tree.","Teams receive mixed quotation cards from one text.","They place each card on BEFORE, TURN or AFTER.","They explain the cause of the change at the TURN.","They choose the best theme card and defend it.","Correct card = 1 point; correct theme with evidence = 3 points."],"data":[]},"exit_quiz":[{"q":"What is a *turning point*?","answer":"the moment when a character begins to change","explanation":"Definition.","explanation_ar":"نقطة التحول.","options":["the moment when a character begins to change","the title of a poem","the last word of a story","a list of characters"],"dok":1,"skill":"character change"},{"q":"Who is the *speaker* of a poem?","answer":"the voice that talks in the poem","explanation":"Definition.","explanation_ar":"المتكلم.","options":["the person who prints the poem","the reader","the voice that talks in the poem","the teacher"],"dok":1,"skill":"point of view"},{"q":"*I touched the rough **bark** of the palm.* *Bark* means ___.","answer":"the hard outer covering of a tree","explanation":"Multiple meanings + context.","explanation_ar":"لحاء.","options":["the sound a dog makes","the hard outer covering of a tree","a small boat","a kind of fruit"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary in context"},{"q":"What is a *wadi*?","answer":"a desert valley that is dry except when it rains","explanation":"Vocabulary.","explanation_ar":"وادٍ.","options":["a desert valley that is dry except when it rains","a tall palm tree","a city street","a mud house"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary"},{"q":"Which sentence about the speaker is correct?","answer":"At first, he was ashamed of his roots.","explanation":"ashamed of.","explanation_ar":"صحيحة.","options":["At first, he was ashamed of his roots.","At first, he was ashamed from his roots.","At first, he was ashamed on his roots.","At first, he ashamed of his roots."],"dok":2,"skill":"grammar"},{"q":"What CAUSED the speaker of *Roots* to change?","answer":"his grandfather's words at the date palm during a visit","explanation":"Cause.","explanation_ar":"كلمات الجد عند النخلة.","options":["a test at school","his grandfather's words at the date palm during a visit","new shoes","a song on his phone"],"dok":2,"skill":"character change"},{"q":"What does the simile *my family name, / shining like new gold* show?","answer":"He now sees his family name as precious.","explanation":"Simile.","explanation_ar":"يرى اسمه ثمينًا.","options":["His name is written in gold.","He wants to sell his name.","His name is very new.","He now sees his family name as precious."],"dok":2,"skill":"figurative language"},{"q":"How are Lulwa and the speaker of *Roots* ALIKE?","answer":"Both learn to value their roots through a grandparent.","explanation":"Compare.","explanation_ar":"كلاهما تعلّم تقدير جذوره عبر جدّ.","options":["Both live in villages.","Both make coffee at Eid.","Both move to the city.","Both learn to value their roots through a grandparent."],"dok":2,"skill":"comparing texts"},{"q":"Why does the poet end with the image of a tree holding on to the earth?","answer":"to give a powerful final picture of the theme: we are strongest when connected to our roots","explanation":"Author's craft.","explanation_ar":"صورة ختامية قوية للفكرة.","options":["to describe farming","to change the topic","to give a powerful final picture of the theme: we are strongest when connected to our roots","to show the weather"],"dok":3,"skill":"author's craft"},{"q":"How does a character's change help readers find the theme?","answer":"What the character learns can become a lesson for everyone.","explanation":"Strategy.","explanation_ar":"ما تتعلمه الشخصية يصبح درسًا للجميع.","options":["It shows the setting.","What the character learns can become a lesson for everyone.","It gives the title.","It lists new words."],"dok":3,"skill":"theme"}],"images":[{"id":"IMG-RW-L07-U1-LS3-V01","prompt":"Picture for the word 'wadi': a dry valley with palm trees"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L07-U1-LS3-V02","prompt":"Picture for the word 'bark': a hand on the rough bark of a palm tree"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L07-U1-LS3-V03","prompt":"Picture for the word 'unroll': a long road stretching across the desert"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L07-U1-LS3-V04","prompt":"Picture for the word 'drift': sound waves floating over houses"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L07-U1-LS3-V05","prompt":"Picture for the word 'speaker': a teenager with a speech bubble full of lines"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L07-U1-LS3-V06","prompt":"Picture for the word 'turning point': a road sign with a curved arrow"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L07-U1-LS3-V07","prompt":"Picture for the word 'ashamed': a boy hiding his face"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L07-U1-LS3-01","prompt":"Memories of a village rising from a box under a bed."},{"id":"IMG-RW-L07-U1-LS3-02","prompt":"A grandfather showing his grandson the roots of a palm."},{"id":"IMG-RW-L07-U1-LS3-03","prompt":"Character change in two texts."}],"audio":[{"id":"AUD-RW-L07-U1-LS3-V01","script":"wadi. The call to prayer drifted over the wadi."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L07-U1-LS3-V02","script":"bark. I touched the rough bark of the palm."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L07-U1-LS3-V03","script":"unroll. The road unrolled like a gray ribbon."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L07-U1-LS3-V04","script":"drift. The sound drifted over the village."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L07-U1-LS3-V05","script":"speaker. The speaker of the poem is a teenager."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L07-U1-LS3-V06","script":"turning point. The visit to the village is the turning point."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L07-U1-LS3-V07","script":"ashamed. At first, the speaker was ashamed of his roots."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L07-U1-LS3-T1","script":"When we moved to the city, / I folded my village into a small box / and pushed it under my bed. / I wanted new shoes, new words, / a voice that sounded like everyone else's. At school, I laughed when they laughed / at the way my grandfather spoke, / and I said my family name quietly, / as if it were a secret / I was tired of keeping. I learned the names of shops and streets, / the songs on everyone's phones, / the fastest way to end a conversation / before anyone could ask / where I was really from. But at night, the box under my bed / was never quite silent. / I heard the palm leaves whispering, / my grandmother calling the goats home, / the call to prayer drifting over the wadi. One summer, my father drove us back. / The road unrolled like a long gray ribbon, / and the city shrank behind us / until it was / a glow in the mirror. The old house was smaller than I remembered, / its mud walls cracked like an old map. / But my grandfather stood at the door / with open arms, / exactly as tall as my memories. He took me to the date palm / his father had planted / the year he was born. / \"Look,\" he said, kneeling in the sand, / \"the roots go deeper than you think.\" \"In a storm, the palm bends,\" he said, / \"but it does not fall. / Do you know why? / Because it never forgets / where it came from.\" I touched the rough bark / and felt something open inside me, / like a door / I had kept closed / for too long. That night, I unpacked the box. / I found my grandmother's songs, / the smell of bread on a clay stove, / and my family name, / shining like new gold. Now, in the city, I speak / my grandfather's Arabic and my classmates' English. / I wear new shoes on old paths, / and when someone asks where I am from, / I answer slowly – and with pride. A tree can grow toward any sky / and drink from any rain, / but it only stands tall / when it holds on to the earth / that holds it."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L07-U1-LS3-VID","script":"When we moved to the city, I folded my village into a small box… At night, the box under my bed was never quite silent… The roots go deeper than you think… it never forgets where it came from. Ashamed → turning point → proud. Lulwa: just an old pot → a bridge. Two changes, one big idea! A tree only stands tall when it holds on to the earth that holds it."}]},{"id":"RW-L07-U1-LS4","title":"Writing Skill: Reflection in personal narrative","lesson_type":"writing_skill","objectives":["tell the difference between events and reflection in a personal narrative","use reflection phrases such as *Looking back, I realize…* and *Now I understand…*","balance events and reflection and end with an insight"],"objectives_ar":["التمييز بين الأحداث والتأمل في السرد الشخصي.","استخدام عبارات التأمل مثل Looking back, I realize… وNow I understand…","الموازنة بين الأحداث والتأمل والختام بفكرة مستخلصة."],"success_criteria":["I can label sentences in Faris's narrative as events, feelings or reflection.","I can write a reflection sentence that compares *then* and *now*.","I can write an insight that explains what an experience taught me."],"success_criteria_ar":["أستطيع تصنيف جمل سرد فارس إلى أحداث أو مشاعر أو تأمل.","أستطيع كتابة جملة تأمل تقارن بين «حينها» و«الآن».","أستطيع كتابة فكرة مستخلصة تشرح ما علّمتني إياه تجربة."],"map_area":"Language Usage: Write, Revise Texts for Purpose and Audience (cross-strand)","vocabulary":[{"word":"reflection","pos":"noun","ipa":"/rɪˈflɛkʃən/","definition":"careful thinking about what an experience means to you","arabic":"تأمّل","example":"The last paragraph is the writer's reflection.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L07-U1-LS4-V01","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L07-U1-LS4-V01","tier":"core"},{"word":"realize","pos":"verb","ipa":"/ˈriəˌlaɪz/","definition":"to suddenly understand something","arabic":"يُدرك","example":"Looking back, I realize I was afraid.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L07-U1-LS4-V02","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L07-U1-LS4-V02","tier":"core"},{"word":"insight","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈɪnˌsaɪt/","definition":"a clear, deep understanding of something","arabic":"فهم عميق / بصيرة","example":"The story ends with a powerful insight.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L07-U1-LS4-V03","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L07-U1-LS4-V03","tier":"core"},{"word":"accent","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈækˌsɛnt/","definition":"the way a person pronounces words, often showing where he or she is from","arabic":"لَكنة","example":"My classmates smiled at my accent.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L07-U1-LS4-V04","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L07-U1-LS4-V04","tier":"core"},{"word":"proverb","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈprɑvɝb/","definition":"a short, well-known saying that gives advice","arabic":"مَثَل","example":"Hamad taught me Arabic proverbs.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L07-U1-LS4-V05","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L07-U1-LS4-V05","tier":"core"},{"word":"gathering","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈɡæðərɪŋ/","definition":"a meeting of people, often family or friends","arabic":"تجمّع / لمّة","example":"Family gatherings were difficult at first.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L07-U1-LS4-V06","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L07-U1-LS4-V06","tier":"core"},{"word":"look back","pos":"phrasal verb","ipa":"/lʊk ˈbæk/","definition":"to think about something that happened in the past","arabic":"يستعيد الماضي / ينظر إلى الوراء","example":"Looking back, I understand my mistake.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L07-U1-LS4-V07","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L07-U1-LS4-V07","tier":"core"}],"explanation":{"text":"A **personal narrative** tells a true story from your life. Strong personal narratives mix two kinds of writing: **events** (what happened – *I sat in the corner with my phone*) and **reflection** (what the experience **means** to you now – *Now I understand that belonging is something you build*).\n\nUse **reflection phrases** to move from the past to the present: *Looking back, I realize… / At the time, I thought…, but now I know… / Now I understand… / This experience taught me… / I will never forget…*. Reflection often **compares** the writer **then** and **now**.\n\n**Balance** is important. A narrative with only events is just a list; a narrative with only reflection is a lecture. Show the events with specific details first, add feelings along the way, then end with an **insight** – a lesson that readers can apply to their own lives – and a **final moment** that shows the change.","examples":["Event: ***My grandmother told long stories, and everyone laughed – everyone except me.***","Feeling during the event: ***I felt like a guest in my own family.***","Reflection phrase: ***Looking back, I realize that my problem was not only the language.***","Then vs. now: ***At the time, I thought I was bad at Arabic, but really I was afraid that I did not belong.***","Insight: ***Belonging is not something you are given; it is something you build, one word at a time.***","Another insight: ***This experience taught me that asking for help is a sign of strength, not weakness.***","Showing the change: ***I told my grandmother a joke in Arabic, and she laughed so hard that she had to wipe her eyes.***","Weak reflection: *It was a good experience.* → Strong: ***Now I understand why my father calls his village 'the place that made me'.***","Balanced paragraph plan: events (P1–P3) → reflection and final moment (P4)."],"common_errors":["✗ Only listing events: *Then… then… then…* → ✓ Add what the experience means to you now. (Events + reflection.)","✗ It was nice. I learned a lot. → ✓ Now I understand that belonging is something you build. (Be specific in reflection.)","✗ Reflecting in every sentence. → ✓ Show the events first; reflect mostly at the end. (Balance.)","✗ Looking back, I realized… → ✓ Looking back, I realize… (*Looking back* + present tense: you realize it **now**.)","✗ At the time I thought I was bad at Arabic but really I was afraid. → ✓ At the time, I thought I was bad at Arabic, but really I was afraid. (Commas after phrases and before *but*.)","✗ Ending with *The end.* → ✓ End with a final moment that shows the change. (Strong ending.)","✗ Giving a lesson to the reader: *You must always…* → ✓ Share what YOU learned: *I learned that…* (Reflection is personal.)"],"arabic":"السرد الشخصي قصة حقيقية من حياتك، ويجمع السرد القوي بين نوعين من الكتابة: الأحداث (ما حدث) والتأمل (ما تعنيه التجربة لك الآن).\n\nاستخدم عبارات التأمل للانتقال من الماضي إلى الحاضر: Looking back, I realize… / At the time, I thought…, but now I know… / Now I understand… / This experience taught me…، والتأمل كثيرًا ما يقارن بين الكاتب حينها والآن.\n\nالتوازن مهم: السرد بالأحداث فقط قائمة، وبالتأمل فقط محاضرة. اعرض الأحداث بتفاصيل محددة، وأضف المشاعر، ثم اختم بفكرة مستخلصة ولحظة أخيرة تُظهر التغيّر.\n\nانتبه: بعد Looking back نستخدم المضارع (I realize) لأنك تدرك ذلك الآن."},"video":{"script":[{"time":"00:00–00:30","narration":"Then… then… then… the end. Is that a story – or a list?","on_screen":"Reflection in a Personal Narrative 🪞","visual":"A boring list of events on a board, then a mirror appears beside it."},{"time":"00:30–01:40","narration":"When my family moved back to Jeddah from Toronto, my Arabic was a disaster…","on_screen":"(model narrative)","visual":"A boy at a family gathering sitting alone with his phone; a cousin teaching him at a table."},{"time":"01:40–02:40","narration":"Looking back, I realize… At the time, I thought…, but really… Now I understand…","on_screen":"Reflection phrases","visual":"A timeline: THEN on the left, NOW on the right, an arrow between them."},{"time":"02:40–03:30","narration":"Events show what happened. Reflection shows what it means. Balance them!","on_screen":"Events + Reflection = Balance ⚖️","visual":"A balance scale with EVENTS and REFLECTION on each side."},{"time":"03:30–04:00","narration":"End with an insight – and one final moment that shows the change.","on_screen":"Insight + final moment","visual":"A grandmother laughing and wiping her eyes; the boy smiling."}],"captions_ar":["ثم… ثم… ثم… النهاية. هل هذه قصة أم قائمة؟","عندما عادت عائلتي إلى جدة من تورونتو، كانت لغتي العربية كارثة…","عندما أنظر إلى الوراء أدرك… في ذلك الوقت ظننت… لكن في الحقيقة… الآن أفهم…","الأحداث تُظهر ما حدث، والتأمل يُظهر ما يعنيه. وازن بينهما!","اختم بفكرة مستخلصة ولحظة أخيرة تُظهر التغيّر."],"voice":"Narrator: American English, thoughtful teenage male voice for Faris; clear teacher voice for the explanation; speed 1.0."},"texts":[{"type":"model","title":"Learning to Speak Like Home (by Faris)","body":"When my family moved back to Jeddah from Toronto, I was twelve years old, and my Arabic was a disaster. I could say *hello* and *thank you* and name a few foods, but that was all. At my new school, I answered every question in English, and I pretended not to care when my classmates smiled at my accent.\n\nAt family gatherings, it was even worse. My grandmother told long stories, and everyone laughed at the right moments – everyone except me. I sat in the corner with my phone, feeling like a guest in my own family.\n\nThen my cousin Hamad had an idea. Every evening after dinner, he gave me a ten-minute lesson. He taught me proverbs, jokes and the words my grandmother loved to use. Sometimes I mixed up two words and said something silly, and we both laughed until we cried. Slowly, the stories at family gatherings began to make sense.\n\nLooking back, I realize that my problem was not only the language. At the time, I thought I was bad at Arabic, but really I was afraid that I did not belong. Now I understand that belonging is not something you are given; it is something you build, one word at a time. Last week, I told my grandmother a joke in Arabic, and she laughed so hard that she had to wipe her eyes. At that moment, I felt completely at home.","word_count":237,"audio_id":"AUD-RW-L07-U1-LS4-T1","id":"TXT-RW-L07-U1-LS4-1","genre":"narrative","role":"model","difficulty":"800L–950L","glossary":[],"annotations":["**Hook and setting:** where, when and the problem – *my Arabic was a disaster*.","**Events in time order** with specific details: *I sat in the corner with my phone*.","**Feelings during the events:** *feeling like a guest in my own family*.","**Turning point:** *Then my cousin Hamad had an idea.*","**Reflection phrases:** *Looking back, I realize… / At the time, I thought…, but really… / Now I understand…*","**Insight – the lesson the writer learned:** *belonging is not something you are given; it is something you build*.","**Strong ending:** a final moment that SHOWS the change – *I felt completely at home*."]}],"activities":[{"id":"RW-L07-U1-LS4-A1","type":"mcq","stage":"controlled","instructions":"Controlled practice: Read Faris's narrative. Choose the answer.","instructions_ar":"اقرأ سرد فارس واختر الإجابة.","items":[{"q":"Where did Faris's family live before moving back to Jeddah?","answer":"Toronto","explanation":"Paragraph 1.","explanation_ar":"تورونتو.","options":["Toronto","London","Riyadh","Dubai"],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"How did Faris answer questions at his new school at first?","answer":"in English","explanation":"Paragraph 1.","explanation_ar":"بالإنجليزية.","options":["in Arabic only","in English","with a nod","in writing"],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"What did Faris do during family gatherings at first?","answer":"He sat in the corner with his phone.","explanation":"Paragraph 2.","explanation_ar":"جلس في الزاوية مع هاتفه.","options":["He told long stories.","He cooked dinner.","He taught his cousins English.","He sat in the corner with his phone."],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"How long was each of Hamad's evening lessons?","answer":"ten minutes","explanation":"Paragraph 3.","explanation_ar":"عشر دقائق.","options":["one hour","ten minutes","thirty minutes","two hours"],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"What did Hamad teach Faris?","answer":"proverbs, jokes and Grandmother's favorite words","explanation":"Paragraph 3.","explanation_ar":"الأمثال والنكات وكلمات الجدة.","options":["math and science","how to drive","proverbs, jokes and Grandmother's favorite words","English grammar"],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"What made Faris's grandmother wipe her eyes?","answer":"She laughed at Faris's joke in Arabic.","explanation":"Paragraph 4.","explanation_ar":"ضحكت من نكتته بالعربية.","options":["She was sad that Faris was leaving.","She had something in her eye.","She laughed at Faris's joke in Arabic.","She was tired after cooking."],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"}]},{"id":"RW-L07-U1-LS4-A2","type":"mcq","stage":"guided","instructions":"Guided practice: Events or reflection? Choose.","instructions_ar":"أحداث أم تأمل؟ اختر.","items":[{"q":"Which sentence from Faris's narrative is an EVENT?","answer":"Every evening after dinner, he gave me a ten-minute lesson.","explanation":"Event.","explanation_ar":"حدث.","options":["Looking back, I realize that my problem was not only the language.","Now I understand that belonging is something you build.","At the time, I thought I was bad at Arabic.","Every evening after dinner, he gave me a ten-minute lesson."],"dok":2,"skill":"events vs. reflection","stage":"guided"},{"q":"Which sentence from Faris's narrative is a REFLECTION?","answer":"Now I understand that belonging is not something you are given.","explanation":"Reflection.","explanation_ar":"تأمل.","options":["My grandmother told long stories.","Now I understand that belonging is not something you are given.","I sat in the corner with my phone.","He taught me proverbs and jokes."],"dok":2,"skill":"events vs. reflection","stage":"guided"},{"q":"Which phrase signals that the writer is moving from the past to the present?","answer":"Looking back,","explanation":"Reflection phrase.","explanation_ar":"عبارة تأمل.","options":["Every evening,","At family gatherings,","Looking back,","When my family moved,"],"dok":2,"skill":"reflection phrases","stage":"guided"},{"q":"Which reflection about joining a new football team is the MOST specific?","answer":"Now I understand that a team is built on trust, not on who scores the most goals.","explanation":"Specific reflection.","explanation_ar":"الأكثر تحديدًا.","options":["Now I understand that a team is built on trust, not on who scores the most goals.","It was a good experience.","I learned a lot that year.","Football is fun."],"dok":2,"skill":"reflection","stage":"guided"},{"q":"Which sentence about Faris's first school days is punctuated correctly?","answer":"At the time, I thought I was bad at Arabic, but really I was afraid.","explanation":"Commas.","explanation_ar":"صحيحة.","options":["At the time, I thought I was bad at Arabic, but really I was afraid.","At the time I thought I was bad at Arabic but really, I was afraid.","At the time, I thought, I was bad at Arabic but really I was afraid.","At the time I thought I was bad at Arabic, but, really I was afraid."],"dok":2,"skill":"punctuation","stage":"guided"},{"q":"Why does Faris end with the joke he told his grandmother?","answer":"It SHOWS his change in a final moment instead of only telling it.","explanation":"Author's craft.","explanation_ar":"يُظهر التغيّر في لحظة أخيرة.","options":["It adds a new problem.","It explains how to tell jokes.","It makes the story longer.","It SHOWS his change in a final moment instead of only telling it."],"dok":3,"skill":"author's craft","stage":"guided"}]},{"id":"RW-L07-U1-LS4-A3","type":"gap_fill","stage":"freer","instructions":"Freer practice: Complete the reflection paragraph with the word bank.","instructions_ar":"أكمل فقرة التأمل من بنك الكلمات.","items":[{"q":"________ back, I realize that moving to a new city made me braver.","answer":"Looking","explanation":"Reflection phrase.","explanation_ar":"عندما أنظر إلى الوراء.","stage":"freer"},{"q":"At the ________, I thought nobody would ever talk to me.","answer":"time","explanation":"Then.","explanation_ar":"في ذلك الوقت.","stage":"freer"},{"q":"Now I ________ that friendships take time to grow.","answer":"understand","explanation":"Now.","explanation_ar":"أفهم.","stage":"freer"},{"q":"This experience ________ me that a smile can open a conversation.","answer":"taught","explanation":"Lesson.","explanation_ar":"علّمني.","stage":"freer"},{"q":"I will never ________ the day my neighbor invited me to play.","answer":"forget","explanation":"Memory.","explanation_ar":"أنسى.","stage":"freer"},{"q":"My biggest ________ is that home is wherever people care about you.","answer":"insight","explanation":"Insight.","explanation_ar":"فكرتي المستخلصة.","stage":"freer"}]},{"id":"RW-L07-U1-LS4-A4","type":"open","stage":"production","instructions":"Writing: Write reflection.","instructions_ar":"اكتب التأمل.","items":[{"q":"Turn this weak reflection into a strong one: *Visiting my grandparents' village was a good experience.*","answer":"Looking back, I realize that the village taught me to slow down and listen, because my grandparents' stories helped me understand where my family's values come from.","explanation":"Specific reflection.","explanation_ar":"تأمل محدد.","dok":2,"skill":"reflection","stage":"production"},{"q":"Write a then-and-now reflection about the first day at a new school.","answer":"At the time, I thought everyone was staring at me, but now I understand that they were just curious and wanted to know me.","explanation":"Then and now.","explanation_ar":"حينها والآن.","dok":2,"skill":"reflection phrases","stage":"production"},{"q":"Write one event sentence and one reflection sentence about learning a family tradition.","answer":"Event: My mother showed me how to make maamoul for Eid, and my first ones fell apart. Reflection: Now I understand that traditions are passed on through patience and practice.","explanation":"Balance.","explanation_ar":"التوازن.","dok":3,"skill":"events vs. reflection","stage":"production"},{"q":"Write a final moment that SHOWS a change in you after learning something about your family.","answer":"Last Eid, I was the one who explained our family tree to my little cousins, and my grandfather nodded at me proudly.","explanation":"Showing change.","explanation_ar":"إظهار التغيّر.","dok":3,"skill":"endings","stage":"production"}]}],"game":{"name":"Event or Reflection?","mechanic":"timed_quiz","rules":["On this screen: answer the lesson questions as fast as you can.","In class: Memory mirror: sentences float up; the child sorts each into EVENT, FEELING or REFLECTION. Weak reflections appear cloudy, and the child chooses the specific version to make the mirror shine.","Teams take turns drawing a sentence card.","They place it in EVENT, FEELING or REFLECTION and explain why.","For an 'upgrade' card, the team rewrites a weak reflection to be specific.","Other teams may challenge an answer.","Correct basket = 1 point; good upgrade = 3 points."],"data":[]},"exit_quiz":[{"q":"What is *reflection* in a personal narrative?","answer":"the writer's thinking about what an experience means now","explanation":"Definition.","explanation_ar":"التأمل.","options":["a list of events in time order","the title of the story","the writer's thinking about what an experience means now","a picture in a mirror"],"dok":1,"skill":"reflection"},{"q":"What is an *insight*?","answer":"a clear, deep understanding of something","explanation":"Vocabulary.","explanation_ar":"فهم عميق.","options":["a funny event","a clear, deep understanding of something","a new character","a spelling mistake"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary"},{"q":"*My classmates smiled at my **accent**.* *Accent* means ___.","answer":"the way a person pronounces words","explanation":"Context.","explanation_ar":"لَكنة.","options":["a kind of clothing","a school subject","a loud noise","the way a person pronounces words"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary in context"},{"q":"Which phrase BEGINS a reflection?","answer":"Now I understand that…","explanation":"Reflection phrase.","explanation_ar":"تبدأ التأمل.","options":["Every morning, I…","Now I understand that…","First, we went…","Then my cousin…"],"dok":1,"skill":"reflection phrases"},{"q":"Which reflection sentence uses the correct tense?","answer":"Looking back, I realize that I was afraid.","explanation":"Tense.","explanation_ar":"صحيحة.","options":["Looking back, I realize that I was afraid.","Looking back, I realized that I am afraid.","Looking back, I am realize that I was afraid.","Looking back, I realizing that I was afraid."],"dok":2,"skill":"grammar"},{"q":"What is the problem with a narrative that has ONLY events?","answer":"It reads like a list and does not show what the experience means.","explanation":"Balance.","explanation_ar":"مجرد قائمة.","options":["It is too short.","It has too many feelings.","It reads like a list and does not show what the experience means.","It needs a title."],"dok":2,"skill":"events vs. reflection"},{"q":"What is the problem with *It was a good experience* as a reflection?","answer":"It is too general; it does not say what was learned.","explanation":"Specific reflection.","explanation_ar":"عامة جدًا.","options":["It is too long.","It has a spelling mistake.","It is an event.","It is too general; it does not say what was learned."],"dok":2,"skill":"reflection"},{"q":"Where does reflection USUALLY appear in a personal narrative?","answer":"mostly near the end, after the events","explanation":"Structure.","explanation_ar":"قرب النهاية غالبًا.","options":["only in the title","mostly near the end, after the events","only in the first sentence","nowhere"],"dok":2,"skill":"structure"},{"q":"Which ending BEST shows that a writer now feels at home in her new school?","answer":"On the last day of term, three classmates asked me to sit with them, and I did not feel like a visitor anymore.","explanation":"Strong ending.","explanation_ar":"أفضل نهاية.","options":["On the last day of term, three classmates asked me to sit with them, and I did not feel like a visitor anymore.","The end.","School is fun.","I went home and slept."],"dok":3,"skill":"endings"},{"q":"How is Faris's insight connected to the unit theme of identity and belonging?","answer":"He learns that belonging is built through effort and connection with family.","explanation":"Theme connection.","explanation_ar":"الانتماء يُبنى بالجهد والصلة.","options":["He learns that Toronto is better than Jeddah.","He learns that languages are not important.","He learns that belonging is built through effort and connection with family.","He learns that jokes are always funny."],"dok":3,"skill":"theme"}],"images":[{"id":"IMG-RW-L07-U1-LS4-V01","prompt":"Picture for the word 'reflection': a girl thinking, with a memory bubble above her head"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L07-U1-LS4-V02","prompt":"Picture for the word 'realize': a light bulb turning on above a boy's head"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L07-U1-LS4-V03","prompt":"Picture for the word 'insight': a key opening a door with light behind it"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L07-U1-LS4-V04","prompt":"Picture for the word 'accent': speech bubbles in different styles"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L07-U1-LS4-V05","prompt":"Picture for the word 'proverb': an old book with a short saying highlighted"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L07-U1-LS4-V06","prompt":"Picture for the word 'gathering': a family sitting together in a large room"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L07-U1-LS4-V07","prompt":"Picture for the word 'look back': a boy looking over his shoulder at a path"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L07-U1-LS4-01","prompt":"A boy feeling left out at a family gathering."},{"id":"IMG-RW-L07-U1-LS4-02","prompt":"Balancing events and reflection."},{"id":"IMG-RW-L07-U1-LS4-03","prompt":"A grandmother laughing at her grandson's joke."}],"audio":[{"id":"AUD-RW-L07-U1-LS4-V01","script":"reflection. The last paragraph is the writer's reflection."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L07-U1-LS4-V02","script":"realize. Looking back, I realize I was afraid."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L07-U1-LS4-V03","script":"insight. The story ends with a powerful insight."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L07-U1-LS4-V04","script":"accent. My classmates smiled at my accent."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L07-U1-LS4-V05","script":"proverb. Hamad taught me Arabic proverbs."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L07-U1-LS4-V06","script":"gathering. Family gatherings were difficult at first."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L07-U1-LS4-V07","script":"look back. Looking back, I understand my mistake."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L07-U1-LS4-T1","script":"When my family moved back to Jeddah from Toronto, I was twelve years old, and my Arabic was a disaster. I could say *hello* and *thank you* and name a few foods, but that was all. At my new school, I answered every question in English, and I pretended not to care when my classmates smiled at my accent. At family gatherings, it was even worse. My grandmother told long stories, and everyone laughed at the right moments – everyone except me. I sat in the corner with my phone, feeling like a guest in my own family. Then my cousin Hamad had an idea. Every evening after dinner, he gave me a ten-minute lesson. He taught me proverbs, jokes and the words my grandmother loved to use. Sometimes I mixed up two words and said something silly, and we both laughed until we cried. Slowly, the stories at family gatherings began to make sense. Looking back, I realize that my problem was not only the language. At the time, I thought I was bad at Arabic, but really I was afraid that I did not belong. Now I understand that belonging is not something you are given; it is something you build, one word at a time. Last week, I told my grandmother a joke in Arabic, and she laughed so hard that she had to wipe her eyes. At that moment, I felt completely at home."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L07-U1-LS4-VID","script":"Then… then… then… the end. Is that a story – or a list? When my family moved back to Jeddah from Toronto, my Arabic was a disaster… Looking back, I realize… At the time, I thought…, but really… Now I understand… Events show what happened. Reflection shows what it means. Balance them! End with an insight – and one final moment that shows the change."}]},{"id":"RW-L07-U1-LS5","title":"Writing Workshop: A four-paragraph personal narrative about an object that matters","lesson_type":"writing_workshop","objectives":["plan a personal narrative about an object that matters with the Object Story Planner","write four paragraphs (200–250 words) that balance description, events and reflection","revise for connotation and reflection, edit and share the narrative in a class Identity Museum"],"objectives_ar":["التخطيط لسرد شخصي عن شيء ذي قيمة بمخطط قصة الشيء.","كتابة أربع فقرات (200–250 كلمة) توازن بين الوصف والأحداث والتأمل.","تحسين الإيحاءات والتأمل وتدقيق السرد ومشاركته في متحف الهوية الصفي."],"success_criteria":["My first paragraph describes the object with sensory details and tells why it matters.","My second and third paragraphs tell the object's story and one key moment with specific details.","My fourth paragraph uses reflection phrases, an insight and a final moment; the narrative is 200–250 words."],"success_criteria_ar":["فقرتي الأولى تصف الشيء بتفاصيل حسية وتذكر سبب أهميته.","فقرتاي الثانية والثالثة تحكيان قصة الشيء ولحظة مهمة بتفاصيل محددة.","فقرتي الرابعة تتضمن عبارات تأمل وفكرة مستخلصة ولحظة أخيرة، والسرد في 200–250 كلمة."],"map_area":"Language Usage: Write, Revise Texts for Purpose and Audience (cross-strand)","vocabulary":[{"word":"heirloom","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈɛrˌlum/","definition":"a valuable object that has belonged to a family for many years","arabic":"إرث عائلي / قطعة موروثة","example":"This dallah is a family heirloom.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L07-U1-LS5-V01","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L07-U1-LS5-V01","tier":"core"},{"word":"keepsake","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈkipˌseɪk/","definition":"a small object that you keep to remember a person or a place","arabic":"تذكار","example":"The stone from the village is my keepsake.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L07-U1-LS5-V02","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L07-U1-LS5-V02","tier":"core"},{"word":"strap","pos":"noun","ipa":"/stræp/","definition":"a narrow piece of leather or cloth that holds something","arabic":"سير / حزام","example":"The watch has a cracked leather strap.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L07-U1-LS5-V03","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L07-U1-LS5-V03","tier":"core"},{"word":"wind","pos":"verb","ipa":"/waɪnd/","definition":"to turn a part of a watch or clock to make it work","arabic":"يُعبّئ (الساعة) / يلفّ","example":"Every morning, I wind the old watch.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L07-U1-LS5-V04","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L07-U1-LS5-V04","tier":"core"},{"word":"punctual","pos":"adjective","ipa":"/ˈpʌŋktʃuəl/","definition":"arriving or doing something at the right time; never late","arabic":"دقيق في المواعيد","example":"My grandfather was always punctual.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L07-U1-LS5-V05","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L07-U1-LS5-V05","tier":"core"},{"word":"responsibility","pos":"noun","ipa":"/rɪˌspɑnsəˈbɪləti/","definition":"a duty to take care of something or someone","arabic":"مسؤولية","example":"The watch is a gift and a responsibility.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L07-U1-LS5-V06","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L07-U1-LS5-V06","tier":"core"},{"word":"treasure","pos":"verb / noun","ipa":"/ˈtrɛʒər/","definition":"to value something very much; something very valuable","arabic":"يعتز بـ / كنز","example":"I treasure my grandfather's watch.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L07-U1-LS5-V07","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L07-U1-LS5-V07","tier":"core"}],"explanation":{"text":"An **object narrative** uses one object to tell a story about **identity** – who you are and where you come from. The object is like a **symbol**: it stands for a person, a value or a memory.\n\n**Paragraph 1 – The object:** describe it with **sensory details** and a strong **hook** that says why it matters (*To anyone else, it is just an old watch. To me, it is…*). **Paragraph 2 – Its story:** who owned it, where it came from and what it shows about them. **Paragraph 3 – A key moment:** one event told with specific details and, if possible, **dialogue**. **Paragraph 4 – Reflection:** use *Looking back, I realize… / At the time, I thought…, but now I understand…*, give an **insight** and end with a **final moment** that shows what the object means to you now.\n\nChoose words with the right **connotations** (*treasured*, not *old and useless*), **balance** events and reflection and keep the narrative **200–250 words**. Use the **past tense** for events and the **present tense** for what you understand now.","examples":["Hook: ***To anyone else, it is just an old watch. To me, it is the most valuable thing I own.***","Sensory details: ***Its leather strap is cracked, and the glass has a tiny scratch across the number nine.***","Its story: ***For forty years, he wore it every day while he worked as a teacher in Abha.***","A memorable saying: ***'A teacher who is late teaches lateness.'***","Key moment with dialogue: ***'Time is the only thing you cannot buy back,' he said. 'Use it well.'***","Reflection: ***Looking back, I realize that he was not giving me a watch; he was giving me his values.***","Then and now: ***At the time, I thought it was just a gift, but now I understand that it is a responsibility.***","Final moment: ***Every morning, I wind it, check the time and think of him.***","Tense check: past for events (*he placed it in my hand*), present for now (*I wind it every morning*)."],"common_errors":["✗ Describing the object without saying why it matters. → ✓ Add a hook: *To me, it is…* (Description + importance.)","✗ Telling the whole history of the family. → ✓ Choose ONE key moment and tell it in detail. (Focus.)","✗ It is an old watch. It is brown. It is small. → ✓ Its cracked leather strap is the color of dark honey. (Sensory details, varied sentences.)","✗ He give me the watch and say use it well. → ✓ He gave me the watch and said, 'Use it well.' (Past tense + punctuated dialogue.)","✗ Ending: *That is my watch. The end.* → ✓ End with a reflection, an insight and a final moment. (Strong ending.)","✗ Using negative connotations by mistake: *my grandfather's cheap old watch*. → ✓ my grandfather's *well-worn* watch (Check connotations.)","✗ Writing 350 words. → ✓ Keep it 200–250 words. (Check the length.)"],"arabic":"سرد الشيء يستخدم شيئًا واحدًا ليحكي قصة عن الهوية: من أنت ومن أين جئت، والشيء هنا كالرمز يدل على شخص أو قيمة أو ذكرى.\n\nالفقرة الأولى: صف الشيء بتفاصيل حسية وجملة افتتاحية تبيّن أهميته. الثانية: قصته (من امتلكه ومن أين جاء). الثالثة: لحظة مهمة واحدة بتفاصيل محددة وحوار إن أمكن. الرابعة: التأمل بعبارات مثل Looking back, I realize… مع فكرة مستخلصة ولحظة أخيرة.\n\nاختر كلمات ذات إيحاءات مناسبة، ووازن بين الأحداث والتأمل، والتزم بطول 200–250 كلمة، واستخدم الماضي للأحداث والمضارع لما تفهمه الآن.\n\nانتبه: ركّز على لحظة واحدة مهمة بدل سرد تاريخ العائلة كله."},"video":{"script":[{"time":"00:00–00:30","narration":"Every family has an object that tells a story. What is yours?","on_screen":"The Object That Tells My Story ⌚","visual":"A shelf of family objects: a watch, a dallah, a key, prayer beads, a photo."},{"time":"00:30–01:20","narration":"Passed down, a gift, something you made, a piece of a place – choose your card!","on_screen":"Idea Cards A–D","visual":"Four cards appear with icons."},{"time":"01:20–02:40","narration":"The watch on my wrist is too big for me, and it runs two minutes slow…","on_screen":"(model narrative)","visual":"An old watch on a boy's wrist; a teacher in Abha checking his watch; a grandfather closing a boy's fingers around the watch."},{"time":"02:40–03:30","narration":"Object. Its story. A key moment. Reflection – four paragraphs, one identity!","on_screen":"P1 Object · P2 Story · P3 Moment · P4 Reflection","visual":"Four museum frames light up one by one."},{"time":"03:30–04:00","narration":"Share your story in our class Identity Museum!","on_screen":"Identity Museum 🏛️","visual":"Students walking past desks with object labels and narratives."}],"captions_ar":["لكل عائلة شيء يحكي قصة. فما شيؤك؟","موروث، أو هدية، أو شيء صنعته، أو قطعة من مكان: اختر بطاقتك!","الساعة على معصمي كبيرة عليّ، وتتأخر دقيقتين…","الشيء، ثم قصته، ثم لحظة مهمة، ثم التأمل: أربع فقرات وهوية واحدة!","شارك قصتك في متحف الهوية الصفي!"],"voice":"Narrator: American English, warm teenage male voice reads the model; encouraging teacher voice for the steps; speed 1.0."},"texts":[{"type":"model","title":"Object Story Idea Cards","body":"**Card A – Passed down through the family** · Examples: a grandparent's watch, a dallah, prayer beads, a ring, an old Quran stand, a handwritten recipe book · Think about: Who owned it first? What did it see? Why was it given to you or your family?\n\n**Card B – A gift that taught me something** · Examples: a book from a teacher, a bicycle from an uncle, a pen from a parent on the first day of school · Think about: Who gave it to you and when? What did they say? What did the gift teach you about yourself?\n\n**Card C – Something I made or earned** · Examples: a medal, a certificate, a model you built, a drawing, a jar of honey you helped to collect · Think about: How hard was it? Who helped you? What does it show about the kind of person you are?\n\n**Card D – A piece of a place** · Examples: a key to an old family house, a stone from your grandparents' village, a photo of a city you left, a bottle of sand from a desert trip · Think about: What place does it connect you to? What memories does it hold? How do you feel when you hold it?","word_count":199,"audio_id":"AUD-RW-L07-U1-LS5-T1","id":"TXT-RW-L07-U1-LS5-1","genre":"idea cards","role":"model","difficulty":"800L–950L","glossary":[],"annotations":["Choose **one object** that matters to you or your family. You will write a **personal narrative** of **four paragraphs**.","Plan: (1) **the object** – describe it with sensory details and say why it matters; (2) **its story** – history and people; (3) **a key moment** – one event told in detail; (4) **reflection** – what you understand now, an **insight** and a **final moment**.","Keep events and reflection **balanced**, use **reflection phrases** and choose words with the right **connotations** to show your feelings."]}],"activities":[{"id":"RW-L07-U1-LS5-A1","type":"mcq","stage":"controlled","instructions":"Controlled practice: Read the idea cards and the model. Choose the answer.","instructions_ar":"اقرأ بطاقات الأفكار والنموذج واختر الإجابة.","items":[{"q":"Which idea card is about objects passed down through the family?","answer":"Card A","explanation":"Cards.","explanation_ar":"البطاقة أ.","options":["Card B","Card C","Card D","Card A"],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"Which object is an example on Card D (a piece of a place)?","answer":"a stone from your grandparents' village","explanation":"Card D.","explanation_ar":"حجر من قرية الأجداد.","options":["a medal","a book from a teacher","a handwritten recipe book","a stone from your grandparents' village"],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"In the model, what is wrong with the glass of the watch?","answer":"It has a tiny scratch across the number nine.","explanation":"Paragraph 1.","explanation_ar":"خدش صغير عند الرقم تسعة.","options":["It has a tiny scratch across the number nine.","It is broken into pieces.","It is missing.","It is painted gold."],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"What was Abu Saleh's job for forty years?","answer":"a teacher in Abha","explanation":"Paragraph 2.","explanation_ar":"معلم في أبها.","options":["a teacher in Abha","a watchmaker in Jeddah","a farmer in Najd","a doctor in Riyadh"],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"When did the grandfather give the watch to the narrator?","answer":"on his eightieth birthday","explanation":"Paragraph 3.","explanation_ar":"في عيد ميلاده الثمانين.","options":["on the narrator's first school day","on his eightieth birthday","at Eid","at a wedding"],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"What does the narrator do with the watch every morning?","answer":"He winds it, checks the time and thinks of his grandfather.","explanation":"Paragraph 4.","explanation_ar":"يعبّئها ويتحقق من الوقت ويتذكر جده.","options":["He puts it in a box.","He shows it to his friends at school.","He winds it, checks the time and thinks of his grandfather.","He sets it two minutes faster."],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"}]},{"id":"RW-L07-U1-LS5-A2","type":"mcq","stage":"guided","instructions":"Guided practice: Plan your object narrative. Choose.","instructions_ar":"خطط لسرد الشيء: اختر.","items":[{"q":"Which is the BEST hook for a narrative about a grandmother's prayer beads?","answer":"To anyone else, they are just old beads. To me, they are the sound of my grandmother's voice.","explanation":"Hook.","explanation_ar":"أفضل افتتاحية.","options":["To anyone else, they are just old beads. To me, they are the sound of my grandmother's voice.","Prayer beads are made of wood.","I have some beads.","This story is about beads."],"dok":2,"skill":"hooks","stage":"guided"},{"q":"Which sentence gives the BEST sensory details about an old house key?","answer":"The heavy iron key is cold in my hand and smells faintly of rust.","explanation":"Sensory details.","explanation_ar":"تفاصيل حسية.","options":["The key is nice.","It is a key to a house.","The heavy iron key is cold in my hand and smells faintly of rust.","I like the key a lot."],"dok":2,"skill":"description","stage":"guided"},{"q":"Which word has the MOST positive connotation for a well-used family object?","answer":"treasured","explanation":"Connotation.","explanation_ar":"إيحاء إيجابي.","options":["treasured","worn-out","useless","ancient junk"],"dok":2,"skill":"connotation","stage":"guided"},{"q":"Which sentence about receiving a gift from an uncle is in the correct tense?","answer":"My uncle handed me the box and said, \"Open it at home.\"","explanation":"Past tense + dialogue.","explanation_ar":"صحيحة.","options":["My uncle hand me the box and say, \"Open it at home.\"","My uncle handing me the box and saying, \"Open it at home.\"","My uncle has hand me the box and said, \"Open it at home.\"","My uncle handed me the box and said, \"Open it at home.\""],"dok":2,"skill":"grammar","stage":"guided"},{"q":"Which paragraph of an object narrative should tell ONE key moment in detail?","answer":"Paragraph 3","explanation":"Structure.","explanation_ar":"الفقرة الثالثة.","options":["Paragraph 1","Paragraph 3","Paragraph 4","the title"],"dok":2,"skill":"structure","stage":"guided"},{"q":"A student's fourth paragraph says only *I like my medal a lot.* What is the BEST advice?","answer":"Add a then-and-now reflection, a specific insight and a final moment.","explanation":"Revising.","explanation_ar":"أضف تأملًا وفكرة ولحظة أخيرة.","options":["Add more facts about the medal's size.","Move it to the first paragraph.","Remove the paragraph.","Add a then-and-now reflection, a specific insight and a final moment."],"dok":3,"skill":"revising","stage":"guided"}]},{"id":"RW-L07-U1-LS5-A3","type":"organizer","stage":"freer","instructions":"Organizer: Plan your narrative with the Object Story Planner.","instructions_ar":"خطط لسردك بمخطط قصة الشيء.","items":[{"q":"The object: write four sensory details about your object.","answer":"a small silver ring; smooth and cool; a tiny crescent engraved inside; it smells of my grandmother's perfume","explanation":"Sensory details.","explanation_ar":"تفاصيل حسية.","dok":2,"skill":"planning","stage":"freer"},{"q":"Hook: write a sentence that shows why the object matters to you.","answer":"To anyone else, it is just a small ring. To me, it is a circle that connects four generations of women in my family.","explanation":"Hook.","explanation_ar":"الافتتاحية.","dok":2,"skill":"planning","stage":"freer"},{"q":"Its story and a key moment: write notes for Paragraphs 2 and 3.","answer":"P2: my great-grandmother's wedding ring from Taif; passed to my grandmother, then my mother. P3: on my thirteenth birthday, my mother slipped it onto my finger and said, 'Now it is your turn to keep our story.'","explanation":"Story + moment.","explanation_ar":"القصة واللحظة.","dok":2,"skill":"planning","stage":"freer"},{"q":"Reflection: write a then-and-now sentence and an insight.","answer":"At the time, I thought it was only jewelry, but now I understand that it is a promise to remember. Insight: the things we keep can keep our families together.","explanation":"Reflection.","explanation_ar":"التأمل.","dok":3,"skill":"planning","stage":"freer"}]},{"id":"RW-L07-U1-LS5-A4","type":"writing","stage":"production","instructions":"Writing: Write, revise, edit and share your narrative.","instructions_ar":"اكتب سردك وحسّنه ودققه وشاركه.","items":[{"q":"Write Paragraph 1 (the object and the hook).","answer":"The ring is so small that it fits only my little finger. It is smooth and cool, and a tiny crescent is engraved inside. To anyone else, it is just a small ring. To me, it is a circle that connects four generations.","explanation":"Paragraph 1.","explanation_ar":"الفقرة الأولى.","dok":2,"skill":"writing","stage":"production"},{"q":"Write Paragraphs 2 and 3 (its story and the key moment).","answer":"It was my great-grandmother's wedding ring. She wore it in Taif for sixty years before she gave it to my grandmother. On my thirteenth birthday, my mother slipped it onto my finger. \"Now it is your turn to keep our story,\" she whispered.","explanation":"Paragraphs 2–3.","explanation_ar":"الفقرتان الثانية والثالثة.","dok":2,"skill":"writing","stage":"production"},{"q":"Write Paragraph 4 (reflection, insight and final moment).","answer":"Looking back, I realize that my mother was not giving me jewelry; she was trusting me with our family's memory. Now I understand that the things we keep can keep us together. Whenever I feel far from home, I touch the crescent and remember four strong women.","explanation":"Paragraph 4.","explanation_ar":"الفقرة الرابعة.","dok":3,"skill":"writing","stage":"production"},{"q":"Write one word you changed to improve its connotation (before → after) and explain why.","answer":"Before: my grandmother's old ring. After: my grandmother's treasured ring. *Treasured* shows love and respect, while *old* sounds unimportant.","explanation":"Revision.","explanation_ar":"تحسين.","dok":3,"skill":"revising","stage":"production","_w":{"checklist":[],"min":null,"max":null}}]},{"id":"RW-L07-U1-LS5-ORG","type":"organizer","organizer":"Object Story Planner","instructions":"Organizer: Choose one card and one object. Plan each paragraph before you draft.","items":[{"q":"The object: sensory details","answer":"too big, runs two minutes slow, cracked leather strap, a scratch across the number nine"},{"q":"Hook: why it matters to me","answer":"To anyone else, it is just an old watch. To me, it is the most valuable thing I own."},{"q":"Its story: who, where, what it shows","answer":"Grandfather Abu Saleh; a teacher in Abha for forty years; never late – 'A teacher who is late teaches lateness.'"},{"q":"A key moment (events + dialogue)","answer":"his eightieth birthday; he closes my fingers around the watch: 'Time is the only thing you cannot buy back. Use it well.'"},{"q":"Reflection: then vs. now","answer":"At the time: just a gift → now: a responsibility"},{"q":"Insight + final moment","answer":"He was giving me his values; every morning I wind it and try to be the person he believed I could be."}]},{"id":"RW-L07-U1-LS5-WR","type":"writing","instructions":"Writing: Choose one Object Story Idea Card and one real object that matters to you or your family. Write a personal narrative (200–250 words) in four paragraphs: (1) describe the object with sensory details and say why it matters; (2) tell the object's story; (3) tell one key moment in detail; (4) reflect on what the object means to you now, with an insight and a final moment. Choose one Object Story Idea Card and one real object that matters to you or your family. Write a personal narrative (200–250 words) in four paragraphs: (1) describe the object with sensory details and say why it matters; (2) tell the object's story; (3) tell one key moment in detail; (4) reflect on what the object means to you now, with an insight and a final moment.","instructions_ar":"اختر بطاقة من بطاقات أفكار قصة الشيء وشيئًا حقيقيًا له قيمة عندك أو عند أسرتك، واكتب سردًا شخصيًا (200–250 كلمة) في أربع فقرات: (1) صف الشيء بتفاصيل حسية واذكر سبب أهميته، (2) احكِ قصة الشيء، (3) احكِ لحظة مهمة واحدة بالتفصيل، (4) تأمّل ما يعنيه الشيء لك الآن مع فكرة مستخلصة ولحظة أخيرة.","prompt":"Choose one Object Story Idea Card and one real object that matters to you or your family. Write a personal narrative (200–250 words) in four paragraphs: (1) describe the object with sensory details and say why it matters; (2) tell the object's story; (3) tell one key moment in detail; (4) reflect on what the object means to you now, with an insight and a final moment.","checklist":["My first paragraph describes the object with sensory details and a hook.","My second paragraph tells the object's story and the people connected to it.","My third paragraph tells ONE key moment with specific details or dialogue.","My fourth paragraph uses reflection phrases (Looking back… / Now I understand…).","I gave a specific insight and a final moment that shows what the object means now.","I chose words with the right connotations to show my feelings.","I used the past tense for events and the present tense for what I understand now.","My narrative is 200–250 words, and I checked spelling and punctuation."],"rubric_id":"RW-RUBRIC-5T","min_words":200,"max_words":275,"organizer":"Object Story Planner (object – hook – its story – key moment – reflection – insight and final moment)","items":[{"q":"Choose one Object Story Idea Card and one real object that matters to you or your family. Write a personal narrative (200–250 words) in four paragraphs: (1) describe the object with sensory details and say why it matters; (2) tell the object's story; (3) tell one key moment in detail; (4) reflect on what the object means to you now, with an insight and a final moment.","answer":"The watch on my wrist is too big for me, and it runs two minutes slow. Its leather strap is cracked, and the glass has a tiny scratch across the number nine. To anyone else, it is just an old watch. To me, it is the most valuable thing I own. (new paragraph) It belonged to my grandfather, Abu Saleh. For forty years, he wore it every day while he worked as a teacher in Abha. Every morning, he checked it before he left the house and said, \"A teacher who is late teaches lateness.\" His former students say he was never late once. (new paragraph) On his eightieth birthday, my grandfather called me to his room. He took off the watch, placed it in my hand and gently closed my fingers around it. \"Time is the only thing you cannot buy back,\" he said. \"Use it well.\" I did not know what to say, so I just nodded. (new paragraph) Looking back, I realize that he was not giving me a watch; he was giving me his values. At the time, I thought it was just a gift, but now I understand that it is a responsibility. Every morning, I wind it, check the time and think of him. I am still late sometimes, but each time I look at the cracked strap, I try a little harder to be the person he believes I can be.","_w":{"checklist":["My first paragraph describes the object with sensory details and a hook.","My second paragraph tells the object's story and the people connected to it.","My third paragraph tells ONE key moment with specific details or dialogue.","My fourth paragraph uses reflection phrases (Looking back… / Now I understand…).","I gave a specific insight and a final moment that shows what the object means now.","I chose words with the right connotations to show my feelings.","I used the past tense for events and the present tense for what I understand now.","My narrative is 200–250 words, and I checked spelling and punctuation."],"min":200,"max":275}}]}],"game":{"name":"Identity Museum","mechanic":"timed_quiz","rules":["On this screen: answer the lesson questions as fast as you can.","In class: Virtual museum: the child builds an exhibit by choosing the best hook, sensory detail, key moment and insight for an object; visitors tap exhibits to collect strong phrases. A full exhibit unlocks a golden frame.","Each student displays the narrative with a museum label.","Students walk the museum with three colors of sticky notes.","They leave a yellow note on a strong hook, a blue note on strong reflection and a green note on a powerful insight, copying the phrase.","Curators (two students) choose the exhibit with the most notes in each color.","Each note received = 1 point; a curator's award = 3 points."],"data":[]},"exit_quiz":[{"q":"What is an *heirloom*?","answer":"a valuable object that has belonged to a family for many years","explanation":"Vocabulary.","explanation_ar":"إرث عائلي.","options":["a new toy","a kind of clock","a valuable object that has belonged to a family for many years","a family photo album only"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary"},{"q":"What is a *keepsake*?","answer":"a small object you keep to remember a person or a place","explanation":"Vocabulary.","explanation_ar":"تذكار.","options":["a small object you keep to remember a person or a place","a safe for money","a kind of game","a school prize"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary"},{"q":"*My grandfather was always **punctual**.* *Punctual* means ___.","answer":"never late","explanation":"Context.","explanation_ar":"دقيق في المواعيد.","options":["never late","very old","very strict","always tired"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary in context"},{"q":"What should the FIRST paragraph of an object narrative do?","answer":"describe the object with sensory details and say why it matters","explanation":"Structure.","explanation_ar":"وصف الشيء وأهميته.","options":["give the insight","list every family member","describe the object with sensory details and say why it matters","tell the ending"],"dok":1,"skill":"structure"},{"q":"Which sentence about the watch uses tenses correctly?","answer":"Grandfather gave me the watch, and now I wind it every morning.","explanation":"Past for events, present for now.","explanation_ar":"صحيحة.","options":["Grandfather give me the watch, and now I wound it every morning.","Grandfather gives me the watch, and now I winded it every morning.","Grandfather giving me the watch, and now I wind it every morning.","Grandfather gave me the watch, and now I wind it every morning."],"dok":2,"skill":"grammar"},{"q":"Why does the writer include the saying *A teacher who is late teaches lateness*?","answer":"to show the grandfather's values and explain why the watch matters","explanation":"Author's craft.","explanation_ar":"لإظهار قيم الجد.","options":["to describe the color of the watch","to show the grandfather's values and explain why the watch matters","to change the topic","to make a joke"],"dok":2,"skill":"author's craft"},{"q":"Which sentence is a REFLECTION, not an event?","answer":"Now I understand that it is a responsibility.","explanation":"Reflection.","explanation_ar":"تأمل.","options":["He took off the watch.","Now I understand that it is a responsibility.","He placed it in my hand.","His former students say he was never late."],"dok":2,"skill":"events vs. reflection"},{"q":"What does the watch SYMBOLIZE in the model narrative?","answer":"the grandfather's values, especially respect for time","explanation":"Symbol.","explanation_ar":"قيم الجد واحترام الوقت.","options":["the price of watches","the city of Abha","the grandfather's values, especially respect for time","the narrator's birthday"],"dok":2,"skill":"symbolism"},{"q":"A partner's narrative tells the whole history of her family in Paragraph 3. What is the BEST advice?","answer":"Choose one key moment and tell it in detail with dialogue.","explanation":"Revising.","explanation_ar":"اختر لحظة واحدة وفصّلها.","options":["Add more dates.","Choose one key moment and tell it in detail with dialogue.","Move it to the title.","Remove all dialogue."],"dok":3,"skill":"revising"},{"q":"How do object narratives connect to the unit question about identity and belonging?","answer":"They show how the things families keep carry values and memories that shape who we are.","explanation":"Theme connection.","explanation_ar":"الأشياء تحمل قيمًا وذكريات تشكّل هويتنا.","options":["They prove that new things are better than old things.","They show that objects are more important than people.","They teach how to repair watches.","They show how the things families keep carry values and memories that shape who we are."],"dok":3,"skill":"theme"}],"images":[{"id":"IMG-RW-L07-U1-LS5-V01","prompt":"Picture for the word 'heirloom': an old ring in a velvet box"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L07-U1-LS5-V02","prompt":"Picture for the word 'keepsake': a small stone on a bookshelf"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L07-U1-LS5-V03","prompt":"Picture for the word 'strap': a watch with a brown leather strap"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L07-U1-LS5-V04","prompt":"Picture for the word 'wind': fingers turning the small knob of a watch"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L07-U1-LS5-V05","prompt":"Picture for the word 'punctual': a man arriving at a door as a clock shows 7:00"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L07-U1-LS5-V06","prompt":"Picture for the word 'responsibility': a boy carefully holding a small box"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L07-U1-LS5-V07","prompt":"Picture for the word 'treasure': a boy holding a watch close to his heart"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L07-U1-LS5-01","prompt":"A class Identity Museum of family objects."},{"id":"IMG-RW-L07-U1-LS5-02","prompt":"Four idea cards for object narratives."},{"id":"IMG-RW-L07-U1-LS5-03","prompt":"A grandfather giving his grandson a watch."}],"audio":[{"id":"AUD-RW-L07-U1-LS5-V01","script":"heirloom. This dallah is a family heirloom."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L07-U1-LS5-V02","script":"keepsake. The stone from the village is my keepsake."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L07-U1-LS5-V03","script":"strap. The watch has a cracked leather strap."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L07-U1-LS5-V04","script":"wind. Every morning, I wind the old watch."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L07-U1-LS5-V05","script":"punctual. My grandfather was always punctual."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L07-U1-LS5-V06","script":"responsibility. The watch is a gift and a responsibility."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L07-U1-LS5-V07","script":"treasure. I treasure my grandfather's watch."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L07-U1-LS5-T1","script":"**Card A – Passed down through the family** · Examples: a grandparent's watch, a dallah, prayer beads, a ring, an old Quran stand, a handwritten recipe book · Think about: Who owned it first? What did it see? Why was it given to you or your family? **Card B – A gift that taught me something** · Examples: a book from a teacher, a bicycle from an uncle, a pen from a parent on the first day of school · Think about: Who gave it to you and when? What did they say? What did the gift teach you about yourself? **Card C – Something I made or earned** · Examples: a medal, a certificate, a model you built, a drawing, a jar of honey you helped to collect · Think about: How hard was it? Who helped you? What does it show about the kind of person you are? **Card D – A piece of a place** · Examples: a key to an old family house, a stone from your grandparents' village, a photo of a city you left, a bottle of sand from a desert trip · Think about: What place does it connect you to? What memories does it hold? How do you feel when you hold it?"},{"id":"AUD-RW-L07-U1-LS5-VID","script":"Every family has an object that tells a story. What is yours? Passed down, a gift, something you made, a piece of a place – choose your card! The watch on my wrist is too big for me, and it runs two minutes slow… Object. Its story. A key moment. Reflection – four paragraphs, one identity! Share your story in our class Identity Museum!"}]}],"unit_test":[{"q":"My grandfather's great-grandfather is one of my ___.","answer":"ancestors","explanation":"Ancestor.","explanation_ar":"أسلافي.","options":["ancestors","heirlooms","accents","proverbs"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary","_part":"Part A – Vocabulary"},{"q":"Badr grew up in a house full of books, and this ___ made him love reading.","answer":"upbringing","explanation":"Upbringing.","explanation_ar":"النشأة.","options":["gathering","spout","upbringing","strap"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary","_part":"Part A – Vocabulary"},{"q":"This silver ring has been in our family for a hundred years; it is a family ___.","answer":"heirloom","explanation":"Heirloom.","explanation_ar":"إرث عائلي.","options":["majlis","wadi","symbol","heirloom"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary","_part":"Part A – Vocabulary"},{"q":"The men sat in the ___ and drank coffee with the guests.","answer":"majlis","explanation":"Majlis.","explanation_ar":"المجلس.","options":["bark","dent","majlis","accent"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary","_part":"Part A – Vocabulary"},{"q":"After the rain, water rushed through the dry ___ outside the village.","answer":"wadi","explanation":"Wadi.","explanation_ar":"الوادي.","options":["spout","wadi","keepsake","insight"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary","_part":"Part A – Vocabulary"},{"q":"*Sara **inherited** her mother's love of poetry.* *Inherited* means ___.","answer":"received from a parent or an older family member","explanation":"Context.","explanation_ar":"ورثت.","options":["forgot completely","sold for money","wrote down quickly","received from a parent or an older family member"],"dok":2,"skill":"vocabulary in context","_part":"Part A – Vocabulary"},{"q":"Which word has the MOST positive connotation for an old family house?","answer":"historic","explanation":"Connotation.","explanation_ar":"إيحاء إيجابي.","options":["shabby","historic","run-down","crumbling"],"dok":2,"skill":"connotation","_part":"Part A – Vocabulary"},{"q":"*The new student **embraced** the customs of his classmates and joined every celebration.* *Embraced* means ___.","answer":"accepted with enthusiasm","explanation":"Context.","explanation_ar":"تقبّلها بحماس.","options":["accepted with enthusiasm","refused to accept","laughed at","ignored"],"dok":2,"skill":"vocabulary in context","_part":"Part A – Vocabulary"},{"q":"Which sentence from a personal narrative is a REFLECTION?","answer":"Now I understand that my name is a gift from my family.","explanation":"Reflection.","explanation_ar":"تأمل.","options":["I wrote my name on the tag.","My teacher clapped.","I arrived at school early.","Now I understand that my name is a gift from my family."],"dok":1,"skill":"events vs. reflection","_part":"Part D – Writing skills"},{"q":"Which phrase is BEST for starting a reflection paragraph?","answer":"Looking back, I realize…","explanation":"Reflection phrase.","explanation_ar":"عبارة تأمل.","options":["Once upon a time…","Looking back, I realize…","First, take a pencil…","In conclusion, the data shows…"],"dok":1,"skill":"reflection phrases","_part":"Part D – Writing skills"},{"q":"Which hook is BEST for a narrative about a grandmother's old sewing box?","answer":"To anyone else, it is just a dusty box. To me, it holds my grandmother's patience.","explanation":"Hook.","explanation_ar":"أفضل افتتاحية.","options":["This is a box.","My grandmother has a box for sewing.","Boxes can be useful for many things.","To anyone else, it is just a dusty box. To me, it holds my grandmother's patience."],"dok":2,"skill":"hooks","_part":"Part D – Writing skills"},{"q":"Which sentence about learning the family tree uses tenses correctly?","answer":"Last summer, my uncle showed me our family tree, and now I keep a copy in my room.","explanation":"Tenses.","explanation_ar":"صحيحة.","options":["Last summer, my uncle shows me our family tree, and now I kept a copy in my room.","Last summer, my uncle showing me our family tree, and now I keep a copy in my room.","Last summer, my uncle showed me our family tree, and now I keep a copy in my room.","Last summer, my uncle has show me our family tree, and now I keep a copy in my room."],"dok":2,"skill":"grammar","_part":"Part D – Writing skills"},{"q":"Which word choice shows the MOST respect for an elderly neighbor?","answer":"wise","explanation":"Connotation.","explanation_ar":"يُظهر الاحترام.","options":["slow","old-fashioned","wise","fussy"],"dok":2,"skill":"connotation","_part":"Part D – Writing skills"},{"q":"A student's personal narrative has four paragraphs of events and no reflection. What is the BEST advice?","answer":"Add a final paragraph that explains what the experience means now, with an insight.","explanation":"Revising.","explanation_ar":"أضف فقرة تأمل وفكرة مستخلصة.","options":["Add a final paragraph that explains what the experience means now, with an insight.","Add a fifth paragraph of events.","Remove the dialogue.","Change it into a poem."],"dok":3,"skill":"revising","_part":"Part D – Writing skills"}],"unit_writing":{"task":"Write a personal narrative (200–250 words) about a time you learned something important about your family's roots – for example, the meaning of your name, a family tradition or the place your family comes from. Write four paragraphs: (1) introduce the topic with a hook; (2) give the background; (3) tell one key moment in detail; (4) reflect on what you understand now, with an insight and a final moment.","model":"For most of my life, I knew my grandparents' names, but not much more. I thought family history was something for old people and museums. That changed last summer in my uncle's house in Buraydah. (new paragraph) One evening, my uncle opened a drawer and took out a long rolled paper. When he unrolled it on the carpet, I saw hundreds of names connected by thin lines, like the branches of a tree. \"This is our family tree,\" he said. \"It goes back nine generations.\" (new paragraph) He pointed to a name near the top: Ibrahim. \"He dug wells for travelers,\" my uncle explained. \"People say that no one who reached his well was ever turned away.\" Then he moved his finger slowly down the branches until it stopped at the bottom. There, in fresh blue ink, was my own name. (new paragraph) Looking back, I realize that I had always thought of myself as one person standing alone. Now I understand that I am one small branch on a very old tree. At the time, the names were just ink on paper, but now they feel like a promise. Whenever a guest knocks at our door, I remember Ibrahim's well, and I make sure that no one is ever turned away.","marks":20}});
